ique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 23 = \\dfrac 2x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 32 = \\dfrac x2\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3}{2} &= \\frac{x}{2} \\\\\n2 \\cdot \\frac{3}{2} &= 2 \\cdot \\frac{x}{2} \\\\\n2 \\cdot \\frac{3}{2} &= 2 \\cdot \\frac{x}{2} \\\\\nx &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 false] [17592186059520 1071 "Solve the equation $\\dfrac 1{x+1} = 2$." 13194139535437 17592186045417 false] [17592186059520 1071 "Solve the equation $\\dfrac 1{x+1} = 2.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186059520 1074 "First, we note that $x=-1$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b} = \\dfrac{c}{d}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac ba =\\dfrac dc\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1{x+1} = 2 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac {x+1}{1} = \\dfrac 12\n$$\n\nThe above equation simplifies to\n\n$$\nx+1 = \\dfrac 12\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the addition principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + 1 &= \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\nx + 1 - 1 &= \\frac{1}{2} - 1 \\\\\nx &= -\\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 false] [17592186059520 1074 "First, we note that $x=-1$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b} = \\dfrac{c}{d}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac ba =\\dfrac dc\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1{x+1} = 2 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac {x+1}{1} = \\dfrac 12\n$$\n\nThe above equation simplifies to\n\n$$\nx+1 = \\dfrac 12 .\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the addition principle:\n\n![](images/d7/d7150c6093121f9f6dc0ecd9675d5be8f0855d339f3404d7a357875362d9990f.svg)" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186059521 1071 "Solve the equation $\\dfrac 1{x-2} = \\dfrac 3{5x}$." 13194139535437 17592186045417 false] [17592186059521 1071 "Solve the equation $\\dfrac 1{x-2} = \\dfrac 3{5x}.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186059521 1074 "First, we note that\n\n- $x=2$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero, and\n\n- $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b} = \\dfrac{c}{d}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac ba =\\dfrac dc\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1{x-2} = \\dfrac 3{5x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac {x-2}{1} = \\dfrac{5x}{3}\n$$\n\nThe above equation simplifies to \n\n$$\nx-2 = \\dfrac{5x}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot (x-2) & =3\\cdot \\frac{5x}{3} \\\\ 3x-6 & =5x \\\\ 3x-5x & =6 \\\\ -2x & =6 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186059521 1074 "First, we note that\n\n- $x=2$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero, and\n- $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b} = \\dfrac{c}{d}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac ba =\\dfrac dc\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1{x-2} = \\dfrac 3{5x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac {x-2}{1} = \\dfrac{5x}{3}\n$$\n\nThe above equation simplifies to\n\n$$\nx-2 = \\dfrac{5x}{3}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3 \\cdot (x - 2) &= 3 \\cdot \\frac{5x}{3} \\\\\n3x - 6 &= 5x \\\\\n3x - 5x &= 6 \\\\\n- 2x &= 6 \\\\\nx &= -3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 false] [17592186059522 1071 "Solve the equation $\\dfrac 1{2x-1} = \\dfrac{1}{x+1}$." 13194139535437 17592186045417 false] [17592186059522 1071 "Solve the equation $\\dfrac 1{2x-1} = \\dfrac{1}{x+1}.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186059522 1074 "First, we note that\n\n- $x=\\dfrac 12$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero, and\n\n- $x=-1$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b} = \\dfrac{c}{d}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac ba =\\dfrac dc\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1{2x-1} = \\dfrac{1}{x+1} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{2x-1}{1} = \\dfrac{x+1}1\n$$\n\nThe above equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n2x-1 = x+1.\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-1 & =x+1 \\\\ 2x-x & =1+1 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186059522 1074 "First, we note that\n\n- $x=\\dfrac 12$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero, and\n- $x=-1$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b} = \\dfrac{c}{d}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac ba =\\dfrac dc\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1{2x-1} = \\dfrac{1}{x+1} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{2x-1}{1} = \\dfrac{x+1}1\n$$\n\nThe above equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n2x-1 = x+1\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x - 1 &= x + 1 \\\\\n2x - x &= 1 + 1 \\\\\nx &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 false] [17592186059706 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503715 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186059707 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503666 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186059707 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503684 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186059708 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503661 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186059708 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503729 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186059709 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503706 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186059941 1050 420.0 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186060371 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186060371 1071 "Solve the equation $-\\frac{1}{2x} = \\frac{3}{5}$." 13194139535437 17592186045417 false] [17592186060371 1071 "Solve the equation $-\\frac{1}{2x}=\\frac{3}{5}.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186060371 1074 "First, we note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{2x}{1}=\\frac{5}{3}\n$$\n\nThe above equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n-2x=\\frac{5}{3}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x & =\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ (-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (-2x) & =(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot \\frac{5}{3} \\\\ \\cancel{(-\\frac{1}{2})}\\cdot (\\cancel{-2}x) & =-\\frac{5}{6} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186060374 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186060374 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{6}{x - 3} = \\frac{2}{x + 6}$." 13194139535437 17592186045417 false] [17592186060374 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{6}{x-3}=\\frac{2}{x+6}.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186060374 1074 "First, we note that\n\n- $x=3$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero\n\n- $x=-6$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{6}=\\frac{x+6}{2}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator, $6.$ The equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{6}=\\frac{3x+18}{6}.\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\cdot \\frac{x-3}{6} & =6\\cdot \\frac{3x+18}{6} \\\\ x-3 & =3x+18 \\\\ x-3x & =18+3 \\\\ -2x & =21 \\\\ (-\\frac{1}{2})(-2x) & =(-\\frac{1}{2})21 \\\\ (-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{2}})(-\\cancel{2}x) & =(-\\frac{1}{2})21 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{21}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186060377 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186060377 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{3}{2} = \\frac{1}{x + 1}$." 13194139535437 17592186045417 false] [17592186060377 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{1}{x+1}.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186060377 1074 "First, we note that $x=-1$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}=\\frac{x+1}{1}\n$$\n\nThe above equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}=x+1\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the addition principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & =x+1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}-1 & =x+1-1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the solution is $x=-\\frac{1}{3}.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186060937 1064 "Remember that to solve a rational equation, such as the one below, we can use cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2}{x} &= \\frac{3}{5} \\\\\n5 \\cdot 2 &= 3 \\cdot x \\\\\n10 &= 3x \\\\\n\\frac{10}{3} &= x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, there is also another method that we can use, known as the **flip method,** which involves taking the reciprocal of both sides of an equation. So, when an equation involves two fractions, we can \"flip\" them as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b} = \\dfrac{c}{d}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac ba =\\dfrac dc\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nThe flip method is useful because it allows us to remove a variable from a denominator. For example, applying the flip method, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}2}{\\color{blue}x}=\\dfrac{\\color{blue}3}{\\color{red}5} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\color{blue}x}{\\color{red}2} = \\dfrac{\\color{red}5}{\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the resulting equation using the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 \\cdot \\frac{x}{2} &= 2 \\cdot \\frac{5}{3} \\\\\n2 \\cdot \\frac{x}{2} &= 2 \\cdot \\frac{5}{3} \\\\\nx &= \\frac{10}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 false] [17592186060937 1064 "Remember that to solve a rational equation, such as the one below, we can use cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x} & =\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 5\\cdot 2 & =3\\cdot x \\\\ 10 & =3x \\\\ \\frac{10}{3} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, there is also another method that we can use, known as the **flip method,** which involves taking the reciprocal of both sides of an equation. So, when an equation involves two fractions, we can \"flip\" them as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b} = \\dfrac{c}{d}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac ba =\\dfrac dc\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nThe flip method is useful because it allows us to remove a variable from a denominator. For example, applying the flip method, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}2}{\\color{blue}x}=\\dfrac{\\color{blue}3}{\\color{red}5}  \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\color{blue}x}{\\color{red}2} = \\dfrac{\\color{red}5}{\\color{blue}3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the resulting equation using the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot \\frac{x}{2} & =2\\cdot \\frac{5}{3} \\\\ \\cancel{2}\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\cancel{2}} & =2\\cdot \\frac{5}{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{10}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186066552 1075 #edb/ref 17592186066555 13194139535437 17592186045417 false] [17592186066552 1075 #edb/ref 17592186066557 13194139535437 17592186045417 false] [17592186066552 1075 #edb/ref 17592186503050 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186066552 1075 #edb/ref 17592186503402 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186066552 1078 #edb/ref 17592186066554 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186068068 1075 #edb/ref 17592186068069 13194139535437 17592186045417 false] [17592186068068 1075 #edb/ref 17592186068070 13194139535437 17592186045417 false] [17592186068068 1075 #edb/ref 17592186068071 13194139535437 17592186045417 false] [17592186068068 1075 #edb/ref 17592186068072 13194139535437 17592186045417 fals