herefore, the final answer is\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n4 \\cdot |x| &=\\[5pt]\n4 \\cdot x &=\\[5pt]\n4x.\t\n\t}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186068444 1071 "Simplify the expression $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{45a^4}}{\\sqrt{5}}$." 13194139535437 17592186045417 false] [17592186068444 1071 "Simplify the expression $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{45a^4}}{\\sqrt{5}}.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186068444 1074 "First, we bring the quotient under a single radical and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sqrt{45a^4}}{\\sqrt{5}}  = \\sqrt{\\dfrac {45a^4} {5} }= \\sqrt{9a^4}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the expression inside the radical is a perfect square. So, we can continue simplifying by writing the expression as this perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{9{a}^{4}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(3{a}^{2})^{2}} & = \\\\ |3{a}^{2}| & = \\\\ |3|\\cdot |{a}^{2}| & = \\\\ 3\\cdot |{a}^{2}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA squared number is always positive or zero, and the absolute value of any positive (or zero) number is itself, so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot |{a}^{2}| & = \\\\ 3\\cdot {a}^{2} & = \\\\ 3{a}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186068444 1074 "First, we bring the quotient under a single radical and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sqrt{45a^4}}{\\sqrt{5}} = \\sqrt{\\dfrac {45a^4} {5}}= \\sqrt{9a^4}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the expression inside the radical is a perfect square. So, we can continue simplifying by writing the expression as this perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{9a^{4}} &=  \\\\\n\\sqrt{(3a^{2})^{2}} &=  \\\\\n\\mid 3a^{2} \\mid &=  \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}3 & \\cdot & a^{2}\\end{vmatrix} &=  \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot \\mid a^{2} \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA squared number is always positive or zero, and the absolute value of any positive (or zero) number is itself, so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3 \\cdot \\mid a^{2} \\mid &=  \\\\\n3 \\cdot a^{2} &=  \\\\\n&= 3a^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 