 1071 "Determine the equation of the line that passes through the points $(2, 7)$ and $(3, 11)$." 13194139535437 17592186045417 false] [17592186069483 1071 "Determine the equation of the line that passes through the points $(2,7)$ and $(3,11).$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186069483 1074 "We will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$ So, we need to work out the slope $m$ and the $y$-intercept $b.$\n\nFirst, we calculate the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{11-7}{3-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{1} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the slope $m=4$ into the slope-intercept formula $y=mx+b,$ we reach\n\n$$\ny=4x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can find this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(2,7):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4x+b \\\\ 7 & =4(2)+b \\\\ 7 & =8+b \\\\ 7-8 & =b \\\\ -1 & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the equation of the line is $y=4x-1.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186069998 1064 "To simplify radical expressions, we can use the quotient rule for radicals, which states\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\sqrt {a}} {\\sqrt{b}} = \\sqrt{\\dfrac a b}\n$$\n\nFor example, let's simplify\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\dfrac{16w^2}{25}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we put the numerator and denominator under separate radicals and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\dfrac{16w^2}{25}} = \\dfrac {\\sqrt{16w^2}} {\\color{blue}\\sqrt{25}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{16w^2}}{\\color{blue}\\sqrt{5^2}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{16w^2}}{\\color{blue}5}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the expression inside the radical is a perfect square. So, we can continue simplifying by writing the expression under the radical as a perfect square and taking its square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\sqrt{16w^{2}}}{5} &=  \\\\\n(\\sqrt{(4w)^{2}})/(5) &=  \\\\\n\\frac{\\mid 4w\\begin{vmatrix}}{5} &= \\\\ \\frac{\\begin{vmatrix}4 & \\cdot & w\\end{vmatrix}}{5}|= \\\\ \\frac{4 \\cdot \\mid w \\mid}{5}|= \\\\ \\frac{4 \\mid w\\end{vmatrix}}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 false] [17592186069998 1064 "To simplify radical expressions, we can use the quotient rule for radicals, which states \n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\sqrt {a}} {\\sqrt{b}} = \\sqrt{\\dfrac a b}.\n$$\n\nFor example, let's simplify $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{16w^2}{25}}$\n\nFirst, we put the numerator and denominator under separate radicals and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\dfrac{16w^2}{25}}  = \\dfrac {\\sqrt{16w^2}} {\\color{blue}\\sqrt{25}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{16w^2}}{\\color{blue}\\sqrt{5^2}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{16w^2}}{\\color{blue}5}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the expression inside the radical is a perfect square. So, we can continue simplifying by writing the expression under the radical as a perfect square and taking its square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{16{w}^{2}}}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{(4w)^{2}}}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{|4w|}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{|4|\\cdot |w|}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{4\\cdot |w|}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{4|w|}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186069999 1064 "The quotient rule for radicals also works for higher-order roots, like the cube root:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{ \\dfrac a b } = \\dfrac{ \\sqrt[3]{a} }{ \\sqrt[3]{b} }\n$$\n\nIn general, we have the following result:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[n]{ \\dfrac a b } = \\dfrac{ \\sqrt[n]{a} }{ \\sqrt[n]{b} }\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186069999 1064 "The quotient rule for radicals also works for higher-order roots, like the cube root:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac a b} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt[3]{a}}{\\sqrt[3]{b}}\n$$\n\nIn general, we have the following result:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[n]{\\dfrac a b} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt[n]{a}}{\\sqrt[n]{b}}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 false] [17592186073752 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503753 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186078496 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503728 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186078604 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503711 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186091684 1078 #edb/ref 17592186091688 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186092287 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503688 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186092287 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503719 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186092288 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503664 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186092288 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503717 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186092288 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503752 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186092288 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503756 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186092292 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503689 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186092292 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503718 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186092292 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503732 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186092292 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503737 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186092309 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503669 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186092451 1050 600.0 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186092914 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186092914 1071 "$\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{3})=$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186092914 1071 "What is $\\cosh (\\ln \\sqrt{3})$?" 13194139535437 17592186045417 false] [17592186092914 1074 "The definition of $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{3}) & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{3}}+{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{3}}+{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1/\\sqrt{3})}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{3+1}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186093453 1064 "The **hyperbolic functions** are functions defined using the exponential functions $e^x$ and $e^{-x}.$ As we will see at the end of the lesson, hyperbolic functions are closely related to trigonometric functions. \n\nThe **hyperbolic cosine** (abbreviated and pronounced \"cosh\") is defined as\n\n$$\n\\cosh x=  \\dfrac12\\left(e^x+e^{-x}\\right).\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=\\cosh x$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/28/286e0b946edf5c8a8eb23753aaef79812d7c1e7471ab32f43eda81795fb7f47a.png)\n\nThe function $y=\\cosh x$ is not a parabola, although it may look like one.\n\nNote the following properties of $y=\\cosh x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- Its domain is $x\\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\n- Its range is $y \\geq 1.$\n\n- It is an even function.\n\nTo evaluate $\\cosh x$ at some value, say $x=2,$ we simply substitute our value into the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}2=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{2}+{e}^{-2})\\approx 3.7622\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 false] [17592186093453 1064 "The **hyperbolic functions** are functions defined using the exponential functions $e^x$ and $e^{-x}.$ As we will see at the end of the lesson, hyperbolic functions are closely related to trigonometric functions. \n\nThe **hyperbolic cosine** (abbreviated and pronounced \"cosh\") is defined as\n\n$$\n\\cosh x=  \\dfrac12\\left(e^x+e^{-x}\\right).\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=\\cosh x$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/28/286e0b946edf5c8a8eb23753aaef79812d7c1e7471ab32f43eda81795fb7f47a.png)\n\nThe function $y=\\cosh x$ is not a parabola, although it may look like one.\n\nNote the following properties of $y=\\cosh x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- Its domain is $x\\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\n- Its range is $y \\geq 1.$\n\n- It is an even function.\n\nTo evaluate $\\cosh x$ at some value, say $x=2,$ we simply substitute our value into the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{2}+{e}^{-2})≈3.7622\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186093456 1064 "Just as we do with trigonometric functions, we can use the basic hyperbolic functions $\\sinh x$ and $\\cosh x$ to create more hyperbolic functions.\n\nThe **hyperbolic tangent** (pronounced \"tanch\") is defined as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tanh}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sinh}x}{\\operatorname{cosh}x} & =\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\,\\,\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=\\tanh x$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/ac/ac244e57465dc7588094b3b6dae74bfc954f4705be6a2f5edbba2db3e6eeaa9d.png)\n\nNote the following properties of $y=\\tanh x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- Its domain is $x\\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\n- Its range is $y \\in (-1,1).$\n\n- It has horizontal asymptotes $y=\\pm 1.$\n\n- It is an odd function.\n\nWe can write down an equivalent definition of $\\tanh{x}$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $e^x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tanh}x & =\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\cdot \\,\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}-1}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third definition of $\\tanh{x}$ can be found by multiplying the numerator and denominator of our original definition by $e^{-x}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tanh}x & =\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\cdot \\,\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{-x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-2x}}{1+{e}^{-2x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use any definition of $\\tanh x$ that we like for a given situation. However, it's often convenient to use the second or third definition when evaluating a function containing $\\tanh{x},$ because they include fewer exponential terms than the first definition." 13194139535437 17592186045417 false] [17592186093456 1064 "Just as we do with trigonometric functions, we can use the basic hyperbolic functions $\\sinh x$ and $\\cosh x$ to create more hyperbolic functions.\n\nThe **hyperbolic tangent** (pronounced \"tanch\") is defined as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x} & =\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\,\\,\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=\\tanh x$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/ac/ac244e57465dc7588094b3b6dae74bfc954f4705be6a2f5edbba2db3e6eeaa9d.png)\n\nNote the following properties of $y=\\tanh x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- Its domain is $x\\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\n- Its range is $y \\in (-1,1).$\n\n- It has horizontal asymptotes $y=\\pm 1.$\n\n- It is an odd function.\n\nWe can write down an equivalent definition of $\\tanh{x}$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $e^x\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x & =\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\cdot \\,\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}-1}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third definition of $\\tanh{x}$ can be found by multiplying the numerator and denominator of our original definition by $e^{-x}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x & =\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\cdot \\,\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{-x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-2x}}{1+{e}^{-2x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use any definition of $\\tanh x$ that we like for a given situation. However, it's often convenient to use the second or third definition when evaluating a function containing $\\tanh{x},$ because they include fewer exponential terms than the first definition." 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186093459 1064 "We can similarly derive a link between $\\sin\\theta$ and $\\sinh\\theta.$ To do this, let's recall Euler's formula once more.\n\n$$\ne^{\\textrm i\\theta} = \\cos\\theta + \\textrm i\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nWe also have\n\n$$\ne^{-\\textrm i\\theta} = \\cos\\theta - \\textrm i\\sin\