] [17592186129107 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503681 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186129108 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503708 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186129108 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503714 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186129109 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503651 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186129109 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503668 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186129109 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503698 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186129364 1050 420.0 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186129649 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186129649 1071 "If $f(x)$ is given by the table shown below, what are its roots?\n\n| $x$ | $-4$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $16$ | $6$ | $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $0$ | $16$ | $25$ |" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186129649 1071 "If $f(x)$ is given by the table shown below, what are its roots?\n\n| $x$ | $-4$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $16$ | $6$ | $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $0$ | $16$ | $25$ |" 13194139535437 17592186045417 false] [17592186129649 1074 "The roots of the function are the values of $x$ such that $f(x)=0.$ In the given table, we see that $f(-2)=0$ and $f(3)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the roots of the function are $x=-2$ and $x=3.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186129816 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186129816 1071 "What is the root of the function $g(x) = \\frac{x}{2} + 4$?" 13194139535437 17592186045417 false] [17592186129816 1071 "What is the root of the function $g(x)=\\frac{x}{2}+4?$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186129816 1074 "The roots are the solutions to the equation $g(x)=0,$ which we solve as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{x}{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =-4 \\\\ x & =2(-4) \\\\ x & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has the single root $x=-8.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186130482 1064 "The **roots** of a function are the values of the input $x$ for which the function equals zero. For this reason, roots are sometimes also called **zeros**.\n\nAlgebraically, the roots of a function are the solutions for $x$ in the following equation:\n\n$$\nf(x)=0\n$$\n\nFor example, if\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1}{2}x+1\n$$\n\nthen we can find the roots by solving the equation\n\n$$\nf(x) = 0\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{1}{2}x + 1 &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{1}{2}x &= -1 \\\\\nx &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, this function has a single root: $x=-2$.\n\nGraphically, the roots of a function are the $x$-intercepts. For example, for the graph of\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1}{2}x+1\n$$\n\nshown below, we can see that the function intercepts the $x$-axis at $x=-2$.\n\n![](images/a7/a75e8a0ae40c5cc940f9fe2d9194b0dcb5c6db2742e87a6d903dc15c0b970e99.png)\n\nWhen shown on a graph, the roots of a function are also called the $\\boldsymbol x$**-intercepts.**" 13194139535437 17592186045417 false] [17592186130482 1064 "The **roots** of a function are the values of the input $x$ for which the function equals zero. For this reason, roots are sometimes also called **zeros**.\n\nAlgebraically, the roots of a function are the solutions for $x$ in the following equation:\n\n$$\nf(x)=0\n$$\n\nFor example, if $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{2}x+1,$ then we can find the roots by solving the equation $f(x) = 0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x+1 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x & =-1 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, this function has a single root: $x=-2.$\n\nGraphically, the roots of a function are the $x$-intercepts. For example, for the graph of $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{2}x+1$ shown below, we can see that the function intercepts the $x$-axis at $x=-2.$\n\n![](images/a7/a75e8a0ae40c5cc940f9fe2d9194b0dcb5c6db2742e87a6d903dc15c0b970e99.png)\n\nWhen shown on a graph, the roots of a function are also called the $\\boldsymbol x$**-intercepts.**" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186132409 1078 #edb/ref 17592186132410 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186134395 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186134395 1071 "Find $2det(A) + 4det(B)$, where\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & \\sqrt{3} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & \\sqrt{2} & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139535437 17592186045417 false] [17592186134395 1071 "Find $2det(A)+4det(B)$, where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\sqrt{3} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & \\sqrt{2} & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186134395 1074 "Let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- Since $A$ is a triangular matrix, its determinant is given by the product of the elements of the main diagonal: \n\n$$\ndet(A)=1\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot 1=4\n$$\n\n- Since $B$ has two identical rows, we obtain\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}3 & \\sqrt{2} & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,det(B)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $2det(A)+4det(B)=2\\cdot 4+4\\cdot 0=8.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186138429 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503674 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186155402 1075 #edb/ref 17592186155403 13194139535437 17592186045417 false] [17592186155402 1075 #edb/ref 17592186155404 13194139535437 17592186045417 false] [17592186155402 1075 #edb/ref 17592186155405 13194139535437 17592186045417 false] [17592186155402 1075 #edb/ref 17592186155406 13194139535437 17592186045417 false] [17592186155402 1075 #edb/ref 17592186155407 13194139535437 17592186045417 false] [17592186155402 1075 #edb/ref 17592186502992 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186155402 1075 #edb/ref 17592186503094 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186155402 1075 #edb/ref 17592186503248 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186155402 1075 #edb/ref 17592186503305 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186155402 1075 #edb/ref 17592186503308 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186155402 1078 #edb/ref 17592186502992 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186157455 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503705 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186157456 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503671 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186157456 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503700 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186157456 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503742 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186157457 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503659 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186157457 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503723 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186157755 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186157755 1071 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y=x{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, which of the following is a suitable ansatz for the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)?$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186157755 1071 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} + 4y = xe^{2x}$.\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, which of the following is a suitable ansatz for the particular solution $y_{p}(x)$?" 13194139535437 17592186045417 false] [17592186157755 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude." 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186162056 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503675 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186162057 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503707 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186162058 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503655 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186162058 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503657 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186162099 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503755 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186162363 1071 "![](images/f2/f26e876d5234313926a2cbaa8938e32c8a725cf3435ce3113361bfa3dc1b7997.png)\n\nA $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A,$ which of the following is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $25$ of ${A}^{T}A?$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186162363 1071 "![](images/f2/f26e876d5234313926a2cbaa8938e32c8a725cf3435ce3113361bfa3dc1b7997.png)\n\nA $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A,$ which of the following is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $25$ of ${A}^{T}A?$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 false] [17592186162363 1074 "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\,…,{v}_{n}\\}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of ${R}^{n}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}\\ge …\\ge {\\lambda }_{n}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{1},$\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A{v}_{1}||=7={\\sigma }_{1},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{1}={7}^{2}=49,$ and\n\n- $||A{v}_{2}||=5={\\sigma }_{2},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{2}={5}^{2}=25.$\n\nTherefore, an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=25$ of ${A}^{T}A$ is ${v}_{2}.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186162363 1074 "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\,…,{v}_{n}}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of ${R}^{n}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}\\ge …\\ge {\\lambda }_{n}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{1},$\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A{v}_{1}||=7={\\sigma }_{1},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{1}={7}^{2}=49,$ and\n\n- $||A{v}_{2}||=5={\\sigma }_{2},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{2}={5}^{2}=25.$\n\nTherefore, an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=25$ of ${A}^{T}A$ is ${v}_{2}.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 false] [17592186162603 1068 #edb/ref 17592186160910 13194139535437 17592186045417 false] [17592186166736 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503704 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186166736 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503716 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186166737 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503678 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186166737 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503735 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186166738 1038 #edb/ref 17592186