als also works for higher-order roots, like the cube root. In the case of the cube root, we have\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{ab} = \\sqrt[3]{a}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{b}\n$$\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[n]{ab} = \\sqrt[n]{a}\\cdot \\sqrt[n]{b}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 false] [17592186185310 1064 "The product rule for radicals also works for higher-order roots, like the cube root. In the case of the cube root, we have\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{ab} = \\sqrt[3]{a}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{b}.\n$$\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[n]{ab} = \\sqrt[n]{a}\\cdot \\sqrt[n]{b}.\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186185346 1064 "The **product rule for radicals** states that the square root of a product equals the product of the square roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\sqrt{a} \\cdot \\sqrt{b} = \\sqrt{ab} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the product rule to combine radical expressions.\n\nFor example, let's suppose we want to combine the radicals in the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5x} \\cdot \\sqrt{6z}\n$$\n\nTo combine the radicals, we apply the product rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{5x} \\cdot \\sqrt{6z} &=  \\\\\n\\sqrt{5x \\cdot 6z} &=  \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{30xz}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 false] [17592186185346 1064 "The **product rule for radicals** states that the square root of a product equals the product of the square roots:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\sqrt{a} \\cdot \\sqrt{b} = \\sqrt{ab}\n\t}\n$$\n\nWe can use the product rule to combine radical expressions. \n\nFor example, let's suppose we want to combine the radicals in the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5x} \\cdot \\sqrt{6z}\n$$\n\nTo combine the radicals, we apply the product rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5x}\\cdot \\sqrt{6z} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{5x\\cdot 6z} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{30xz} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186192701 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503677 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186192753 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503695 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186198951 1068 #edb/ref 17592186197576 13194139535437 17592186045417 false] [17592186198951 1071 "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{1+(x-y)^{2}}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le x-y\\le 2\\}.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186198951 1071 "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{1+(x-y)^{2}}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le x-y\\le 2}.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 false] [17592186198951 1074 "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le x-y\\le 2,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x-y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{1+(x-y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{1+{v}^{2}}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{1+{v}^{2}}\\,|-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{1+{v}^{2}}\\,du]dv \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{1+{v}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}du]dv \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{1+{v}^{2}}[u]_{0}^{2}\\,dv \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{1+{v}^{2}}\\,dv \\\\  & =2[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡v]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186198951 1074 "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le x-y\\le 2,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change 