 to $P.$ We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =2\\,i-j-k \\\\ (1-t)\\,i-{t}^{2}\\,j+(2t+1)\\,k & =2\\,i-j-k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand equating the coefficients of $i,j,$ and $k$ yields the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}1-t=2 \\\\ -{t}^{2}=-1 \\\\ 2t+1=-1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $t=-1.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(2,-1,-1)$ is at the tip of the vector $r(-1).$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{t}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+1)\\,k \\\\  & =-i-2t\\,j+2\\,k \\\\ {r}^{′}(-1) & =-i-2(-1)\\,j+2\\,k \\\\  & =-i+2\\,j+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the given curve at the point $P(2,-1,-1)$ is $-i+2\\,j+2\\,k.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 false] [17592186269240 1071 "A $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=9$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=2\\sqrt{3},$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186269240 1071 "A $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=9$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=2\\sqrt{3},$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." 13194139535437 17592186045417 false] [17592186269240 1074 "The matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto the ellipse $\\{Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^