onding to the eigenvalue $16$ of ${A}^{T}A?$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186388618 1071 "![](images/7a/7a2aea092b82dd217b848abdfc162425f098bc13c53975ccfced3f60a4e2ebfb.png)\n\nA $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A,$ which of the following is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $16$ of ${A}^{T}A?$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 false] [17592186388618 1074 "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\,…,{v}_{n}\\}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of ${R}^{n}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}\\ge …\\ge {\\lambda }_{n}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{1},$\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A{v}_{1}||=8={\\sigma }_{1},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{1}={8}^{2}=64,$ and\n\n- $||A{v}_{2}||=4={\\sigma }_{2},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{2}={4}^{2}=16.$\n\nTherefore, an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=16$ of ${A}^{T}A$ is ${v}_{2}.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186388618 1074 "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\,…,{v}_{n}}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of ${R}^{n}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}\\ge …\\ge {\\lambda }_{n}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{1},$\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A{v}_{1}||=8={\\sigma }_{1},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{1}={8}^{2}=64,$ and\n\n- $||A{v}_{2}||=4={\\sigma }_{2},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{2}={4}^{2}=16.$\n\nTherefore, an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=16$ of ${A}^{T}A$ is ${v}_{2}.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 false] [17592186410824 1074 "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t-2,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $⟨-1\\,,\\,1⟩$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})=c⟨-1,1⟩.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =c⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\ ⟨2t-2,4⟩ & =c⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\ ⟨2t-2,4⟩ & =⟨-c,c⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2t-2=-c \\\\ 4=c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we obtain that $c=4.$ Substituting $c=4$ into the first equation, we get $t=-1.$ \n\nTherefore, $⟨-1,1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=-1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1) & =⟨(-1)^{2}-2(-1),4(-1)-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,-5⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨-1,1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $(3,-5).$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186410824 1074 "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t-2,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $⟨-1\\,,\\,1⟩$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})=c⟨-1,1⟩.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =c⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\ ⟨2t-2,4⟩ & =c⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\ ⟨2t-2,4⟩ & =⟨-c,c⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2t-2=-c \\\\ 4=c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we obtain that $c=4.$ Subst