7592186503647 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503710 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503647 1039 "Finding an Unknown Complementary Angle" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503647 1041 #edb/ref 17592186503730 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503647 1042 #uuid "e79fca72-ad43-5c9b-94cd-57daec56de76" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503648 1084 #edb/ref 17592186503762 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503648 1084 #edb/ref 17592186503793 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503648 1084 #edb/ref 17592186503795 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503648 1084 #edb/ref 17592186503796 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503648 1084 #edb/ref 17592186503809 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503648 1084 #edb/ref 17592186503823 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503648 1084 #edb/ref 17592186503837 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503648 1084 #edb/ref 17592186503838 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503648 1085 "Mary and her little brother Sam live together. Their house is located $960$ meters from the community park. The following graph shows Mary's distance from home as she walks home from the park.\n\n![](images/73/733489f3198f4b26719b2ad770eae3d965f8f008f4a8171ae67c837af68436e0.png)" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503648 1086 #uuid "0193adbb-a9ed-50c0-8c39-bfe4319c7952" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503648 1087 #edb/ref 17592186503795 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503649 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503521 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503649 1069 "q-37876" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503649 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503649 1071 "![](images/ae/ae3fdc9540bdca476e2f0dc06bf55826daade1ff57bad3b355eb2981a2acff57.png)\n\nWhat are the roots of the function shown above?" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503649 1072 #uuid "0645f9ac-fa94-5fef-b4fc-1a67ee7ebbe9" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503649 1074 "The roots of the function are the $x$-intercepts. Therefore, the roots are $x=-4,-2,0,1,4.$\n\n![](images/2d/2d39c0f73b96e6d9a8e5208ac96dc36d215bc8e9217438ae3f5a334ac0e762d5.png)" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503650 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503575 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503650 1069 "q-162926" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503650 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503650 1071 "$\\sqrt{\\frac{4{z}^{4}}{49}}=$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503650 1072 #uuid "0691dce4-a0cf-573f-ba88-5eaf489529db" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503650 1074 "First, we put the numerator and denominator under separate radicals and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\frac{4{z}^{4}}{49}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{4{z}^{4}}}{\\sqrt{49}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{4{z}^{4}}}{\\sqrt{{7}^{2}}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{4{z}^{4}}}{7}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the expression inside the radical is a perfect square. So, we can continue simplifying by writing the expression as this perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{4{z}^{4}}}{7} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{(2{z}^{2})^{2}}}{7} & = \\\\ \\frac{|2{z}^{2}|}{7} & = \\\\ \\frac{|2|\\cdot |{z}^{2}|}{7} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot |{z}^{2}|}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA squared number is always positive or zero, and the absolute value of any positive (or zero) number is itself, so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\cdot |{z}^{2}|}{7} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot {z}^{2}}{7} & = \\\\ \\frac{2{z}^{2}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503651 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503527 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503651 1069 "q-217139" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503651 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503651 1071 "The root of the function $g(x)=\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{x}{15}$ is $x=${{field-1}}." 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503651 1072 #uuid "093b2e5c-e9f3-501b-a863-23c715264f2d" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503651 1074 "The roots are the solutions to the equation $g(x)=0,$ which we solve as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{x}{15} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{x}{15} & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{60}{3} \\\\ x & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has the single root $x=20.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503652 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503565 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503652 1069 "q-5920" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503652 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503652 1071 "![](images/41/41719768d5ad07b128d4a44d0dd767c8b40ca23d4a2cc4ea7179c1591c900ebd.png)\n\nSolve for $x$ given the figure above." 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503652 1072 #uuid "0c6ac92e-0be2-531f-8b8a-397c09e6a538" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503652 1074 "Since the two angles are supplementary, the sum of their measures is equal to ${180}^{∘}.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{40}^{∘}+(5x+{20}^{∘}) & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 5x+{60}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 5x & ={120}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={24}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503653 1069 "e-18991" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503653 1071 "![](images/29/29559fd9cbee104e92c719d4c33dd7560d2e2ab8c4f25490659b73f07350debc.png)\n\nSolve for $x$ in the figure above given that $\\angle AOC$ is complementary to $\\angle COD.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503653 1072 #uuid "0d68be9a-8e75-5378-a88f-92da1a8cda66" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503653 1074 "From the diagram, we have \n\n$$\nm\\angle AOC=x + 24^{\\circ}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\nm\\angle COD=3x.\n$$\n\n  \n\nSince $\\angle AOC$ is complementary to $\\angle COD,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+{24}^{∘})+3x & ={90}^{∘} \\\\ 4x+{24}^{∘} & ={90}^{∘} \\\\ 4x & ={66}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={16.5}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503654 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503535 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503654 1069 "q-79412" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503654 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503654 1071 "![](images/3c/3cff4fddd4ae530a233a4f667b3fc84cc2109914a59dda332cbcc8bd5838a516.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $y=f(x)$, is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{″}(x)?$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503654 1072 #uuid "0ea6bbbd-4b7d-5900-9086-5360a21a6636" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503654 1074 "First, if we denote $g(x)={f}^{′}(x)$ then ${f}^{″}(x)={g}^{′}(x).$ So, we need to sketch the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$ given the graph of $y=g(x),$ as usual.\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=g(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have ${g}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- When $g(x)$ is increasing, we have ${g}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $g(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${g}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the stationary points were identified (approximately) from the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n| ${g}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n\nAdditionally, the function $y=g(x)$ has inflection points at $x≈-0.5$ and $x≈0.5.$ At the inflection points, we have ${g}^{″}(x)=0,$ so the tangent to the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$ must be parallel to the $x$-axis at $x≈-0.5$ and $x≈0.5.$\n\nWe don't know the exact values of ${g}^{′}(-0.5)$ and ${g}^{′}(0.5)$, but from the graph of $y=g(x)$ we find that the slope of the tangents to ${g}^{′}(x)$ at $x≈-0.5$ and $x≈0.5$ are negative and positive, respectively. \n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)={f}^{″}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/b5/b5cdb9170fbf8521bddc184d7a58cf0666ed9ad7d6323407ba1f518c59a9ede9.png)" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503655 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503580 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503655 1069 "q-109092" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503655 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503655 1071 "The probability distribution of the random variable $X$ is given below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]=2,$ calculate $\\text{Var}[\\sqrt{3}X-5].$ \n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503655 1072 #uuid "0fdadb20-0f32-5fc9-95d6-7a680685f826" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503655 1074 "Recall that for any random variable $X$ and constants $a$ and $b,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+b]={a}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=\\sqrt{3}$ and $b=-5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\sqrt{3}X-5] & ={\\sqrt{3}}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the variance of $X$ using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we're given that $\\text{E}[X]=2.$ \n\nWe start by computing $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}].$ Summing up the products of each value of ${X}^{2}$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={0}^{2}\\cdot f(0)+{2}^{2}\\cdot f(2)+{4}^{2}\\cdot f(4) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}+16\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{3}-{2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\sqrt{3}X-5] & =3\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503656 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503604 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503656 1069 "q-162907" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503656 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503656 1071 "$\\sqrt{\\frac{16{d}^{2}}{49}}=$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503656 1072 #uuid "1335ac60-8bcf-5956-af28-bb3838c1f5f0" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503656 1074 "First, we put the numerator and denominator under separate radicals and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\frac{16{d}^{2}}{49}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{16{d}^{2}}}{\\sqrt{49}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{16{d}^{2}}}{\\sqrt{{7}^{2}}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{16{d}^{2}}}{7}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the expression inside the radical is a perfect square. So, we can continue simplifying by writing the expression as this perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{16{d}^{2}}}{7} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{(4d)^{2}}}{7} & = \\\\ \\frac{|4d|}{7} & = \\\\ \\frac{|4|\\cdot |d|}{7} & = \\\\ \\frac{4\\cdot |d|}{7} & = \\\\ \\frac{4|d|}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503657 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503570 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503657 1069 "q-109088" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503657 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503657 1071 "If $\\text{Var}[X]=1,$ calculate $\\text{Var}[7-\\sqrt{5}X].$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503657 1072 #uuid "14c37ea2-4835-508a-983a-3cccd74633e3" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503657 1074 "Recall that for any random variable $X$ and constants $a$ and $b,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+b]={a}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=-\\sqrt{5}$ and $b=7.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[7-\\sqrt{5}X] & ={(-\\sqrt{5})}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503658 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503637 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503658 1069 "q-82180" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503658 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503658 1071 "Which of the following could be the graph of a continuous function $f(x)$ that satisfies the conditions below?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)$ and ${f}^{″}(x)$ are undefined at $x=1$\n\n- if $x\\in (-\\infty ,1)$ then ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ and if $x\\in (1,\\infty )$ then ${f}^{′}(x)>0$\n\n- if $x\\in (-\\infty ,1)$ then ${f}^{″}(x)<0$ and if $x\\in (1,\\infty )$ then ${f}^{″}(x)>0$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503658 1072 #uuid "1508af35-4265-5067-b707-b4b8e57d8d0a" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503658 1074 "We summarize all the given information in the following table:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $\\text{undef.}$ | $+$ |\n| ${f}^{″}$ | $-$ | $\\text{undef.}$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↘\\,\\,⌢$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗\\,\\,⌣$ |\n\nNow, let's sketch our graph: \n\n- We draw a point on the vertical line $x=1$, where the derivative is undefined.\n\n- Since $x=1$ is a local minimum, the graph must go up to the left and to the right of that point.\n\n- The graph must be concave down on $(-\\infty ,1)$ and concave up on $(1,\\infty ).$\n\n- Finally, we are told that the function is continuous.\n\nTherefore, a possible graph of $f$ could be as follows:\n\n![](images/0b/0b08e02b0999da13ec71b2fee4e2df4a8754f54d99cb711e6ff19c7a81ace9de.png)" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503659 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503560 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503659 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503624 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503659 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503626 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503659 1069 "q-337710" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503659 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503659 1071 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y={e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503659 1072 #uuid "16980bbd-b929-5d3b-bedf-b283c32f0104" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503659 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{3x})}^{′}-3(u{e}^{3x}) & ={e}^{3x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3u{e}^{3x}-3u{e}^{3x} & ={e}^{3x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+\\cancel{3u{e}^{3x}}-\\cancel{3u{e}^{3x}} & ={e}^{3x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x} & ={e}^{3x} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =1 \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{3x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{3x} \\\\  & =x{e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503660 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503638 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503660 1069 "q-4292" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503660 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503660 1071 "![](images/3c/3cdb1d5b634920eaf60ac7063f9d13c4e3584a9271ed8ce04b470c7b4cdfc584.png)\n\nWhat angle is supplementary to $∠BFD?$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503660 1072 #uuid "19938b2e-df61-5c1b-94bd-c8376682470c" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503660 1074 "![](images/8c/8c7af8d6b0c71de4bee162ce1475c3c8622716443534df8455c3dcecf082d97d.png)\n\nTwo angles are supplementary if their measures add up to ${180}^{∘},$ a straight angle.\n\nOnly $∠DFE$ is supplementary to $∠BFD$ in the given diagram." 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503661 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503643 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503661 1069 "q-67425" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503661 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503661 1071 "Solve the equation $-\\frac{3}{x}=\\frac{9}{3x-1}.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503661 1072 #uuid "1a235d31-7002-5190-8de9-b78ca920ac5f" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503661 1074 "First, we note that\n\n- $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero\n\n- $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{x}{3}=\\frac{3x-1}{9}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator $9.$ The above equation becomes\n\n$$\n-\\frac{3x}{9}=\\frac{3x-1}{9}.\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-9)\\cdot \\frac{3x}{-9} & =(-9)\\cdot \\frac{3x-1}{9} \\\\ 3x & =(-1)\\cdot (3x-1) \\\\ 3x & =1-3x \\\\ 3x+3x & =1-3x+3x \\\\ 6x & =1 \\\\ (\\frac{1}{6})\\cdot 6x & =(\\frac{1}{6})\\cdot 1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503662 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503578 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503662 1069 "q-98316" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503662 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503662 1071 "Which of the following curves is closed?" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503662 1072 #uuid "1aa311c3-05f2-5ca7-b42c-e09f5b19abbd" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503662 1074 "A curve is closed if its terminal point coincides with its initial point.\n\nOf the given options, the only that satisfies the definition is the following:\n\n![](images/43/4344735e4d5107c0bf46ac6ce4060b588aee2847efa58f828462915266f80b72.png)" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503663 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503582 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503663 1069 "q-127521" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503663 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503663 1071 "![](images/e7/e74f96531b57a877b043ac4302adae6beef90dc24d64efc8e1a24b563e4738b7.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the ellipse shown above." 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503663 1072 #uuid "1e5b95a8-8914-5efe-a3ab-4b5dce1c8cbe" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503663 1074 "Since our ellipse is centered at $(-1,2)$ and has semiaxes $4$ and $2,$ its equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-(-1))^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{{2}^{2}}=1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{16})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2(x+1)}{16}+\\frac{2(y-2)}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{y-2}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{x+1}{8} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{x+1}{4(y-2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503664 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503598 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503664 1069 "q-342673" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503664 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503664 1071 "Simplify $tanh\\,(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)$ using the definition of the hyperbolic tangent.\n\n$tanh\\,(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=$\n\n| {{field-1}} |\n| --- |\n| ${x}^{3}+1$ |" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503664 1072 #uuid "1e63bbce-6bf5-50ed-a823-bd6bd4ad0965" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503664 1074 "The definition of $\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x & =\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}-1}{{e}^{2x}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-2x}}{1+{e}^{-2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the second definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tanh\\,(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) & =\\frac{{e}^{2(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}-1}{{e}^{2(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}-1}{{e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{3}}-1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{3}}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}-1}{{x}^{3}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503665 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503636 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503665 1069 "q-166760" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503665 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503665 1071 "How long did it take Sam to reach the community park from home? Express your answer as a fraction or a decimal.\n\nIt took Sam$\\,$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$minutes to reach the community park" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503665 1072 #uuid "1f3496f7-56e0-5008-8c00-9a987ff3c119" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503665 1074 "Since the community park is $960$ meters from Sam's home, we need to solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{300}{11}t=960.\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply both sides of the equation by $11$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{300}{11}t & =960 \\\\ 11\\cdot \\frac{300}{11}t & =11\\cdot 960 \\\\ \\cancel{11}\\cdot \\frac{300}{\\cancel{11}}t & =11\\cdot 960 \\\\ 300t & =11\\cdot 960\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides of the equation by $300$ to isolate $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}300t & =11\\cdot 960 \\\\ \\frac{300t}{300} & =\\frac{11\\cdot 960}{300} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{300}t}{\\cancel{300}} & =\\frac{11\\cdot 960}{300} \\\\ t & =\\frac{11\\cdot 16\\cdot \\cancel{60}}{5\\cdot \\cancel{60}} \\\\ t & =\\frac{11\\cdot 16}{5} \\\\ t & =\\frac{176}{5}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it took $\\frac{176}{5}=35\\frac{1}{5}$ minutes for Sam to reach the community park." 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503666 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503559 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503666 1069 "q-67419" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503666 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503666 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{2}{5}=\\frac{1}{x+3}.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503666 1072 #uuid "1f68ddcf-7f49-5e13-8fb1-2db21d4acf5a" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503666 1074 "First, we note that $x=-3$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{x+3}{1}\n$$\n\nThe above equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{2}=x+3\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the addition principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2} & =x+3 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}-3 & =x+3-3 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the solution is $x=-\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503667 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503642 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503667 1069 "q-339640" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503667 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503667 1071 "The horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{3}-1}{{x}^{5}-2x}$ is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503667 1072 #uuid "223b3e58-bcca-5fb5-a51e-27d487af6492" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503667 1074 "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{5}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{3}-1}{{x}^{5}-2x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{2x}{{x}^{5}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}})}{(1-\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-0}{1-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$ is $y=0.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503668 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503561 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503668 1069 "q-60890" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503668 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503668 1071 "What is the root of the function $f(x)=3x-6?$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503668 1072 #uuid "24214f4f-5b69-59e3-9894-848806975f44" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503668 1074 "The roots are the solutions to the equation $f(x)=0,$ which we solve as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =0 \\\\ 3x-6 & =0 \\\\ 3x & =6 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has the single root $x=2.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503669 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503591 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503669 1069 "q-138921" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503669 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503669 1071 "Find the real solution to the equation $4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x=-3.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503669 1072 #uuid "28cd517a-376e-5db1-863f-bc38045c2ab7" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503669 1074 "The definition of $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above definition into the equation $4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x=-3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})) & =-3 \\\\ 2({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}) & =-3 \\\\ 2{e}^{x}-2{e}^{-x} & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply both sides of the equation by ${e}^{x},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\cdot (2{e}^{x}-2{e}^{-x}) & ={e}^{x}\\cdot (-3) \\\\ 2{e}^{2x}-2 & =-3{e}^{x} \\\\ 2{e}^{2x}+3{e}^{x}-2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a quadratic equation in ${e}^{x}$ that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2{e}^{x}-1)({e}^{x}+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n2{e}^{x}-1=0,\\,{e}^{x}+2=0\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{x}-1 & =0 &  & \\, & {e}^{x}+2 & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x} & =\\frac{1}{2} &  & \\, & {e}^{x} & =-2 \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) &  & \\, & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no real values of $x$ for which ${e}^{x}=-2.$ Therefore, $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})$ is the only real solution to the equation." 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503670 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503608 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503670 1069 "q-116947" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503670 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503670 1071 "Find the measure of an angle that's supplementary to an angle of $(2x+65)^{∘}.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503670 1072 #uuid "29678fcd-bdef-5e0e-a077-5ea622166f37" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503670 1074 "Two angles are supplementary if their measures add up to ${180}^{∘}.$ \n\nSo, the measure of an angle that's supplementary to an angle of $(2x+65)^{∘}$ must be\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}-(2x+65)^{∘} & =(180-2x-65)^{∘} \\\\  & =(180-65-2x)^{∘} \\\\  & =(115-2x)^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503671 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503534 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503671 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503539 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503671 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503577 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503671 1069 "q-352901" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503671 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503671 1071 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503671 1072 #uuid "2f37a13c-db81-5c51-b1fe-cf49ee77cc51" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503671 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{-x})}^{′}+(u{e}^{-x}) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-x}-u{e}^{-x}+u{e}^{-x} & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-x}-\\cancel{u{e}^{-x}}+\\cancel{u{e}^{-x}} & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-x} & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x \\\\ {u}^{′} & =3{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =3{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 3{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =\\int 3{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{-x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =\\int 3{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {e}^{-x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}\\int 3{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503672 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503593 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503672 1069 "q-162911" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503672 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503672 1071 "$\\sqrt{\\frac{81}{16{k}^{2}}}=$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503672 1072 #uuid "31429a2f-5c61-530a-8222-8a7650300604" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503672 1074 "First, we put the numerator and denominator under separate radicals and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\frac{81}{16{k}^{2}}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{81}}{\\sqrt{16{k}^{2}}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{{9}^{2}}}{\\sqrt{16{k}^{2}}}=\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{16{k}^{2}}}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the expression inside the radical is a perfect square. So, we can continue simplifying by writing the expression as this perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{16{k}^{2}}} & = \\\\ \\frac{9}{\\sqrt{(4k)^{2}}} & = \\\\ \\frac{9}{|4k|} & = \\\\ \\frac{9}{|4|\\cdot |k|} & = \\\\ \\frac{9}{4\\cdot |k|} & = \\\\ \\frac{9}{4|k|}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503673 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503551 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503673 1069 "q-141493" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503673 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503673 1071 "The expression $\\sqrt{14u}\\cdot \\sqrt{v}$ is equivalent to" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503673 1072 #uuid "3146291f-928e-5260-bc40-131359b787ef" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503673 1074 "We apply the product rule for radicals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{14u}\\cdot \\sqrt{v} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{14u\\cdot v} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{14uv}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503674 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503524 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503674 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503586 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503674 1069 "q-166758" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503674 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503674 1071 "![](images/ba/ba84a222a38abb930dbf0dde24479abcbfb3ccdca4f50beec0792e90e212d0b0.png)\n\nSam left home to go to the community park at the same time that Mary left the community park to go home. Both Mary's and Sam's distances from home are shown in the graph above. Based on the graph, we conclude that:\n\nMary and Sam met$\\,$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$minutes since they both started moving.\n\nThey met at a distance of$\\,$ \n{{field-2}}$\\,$meters from their home." 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503674 1072 #uuid "31feda6f-3ad0-51f3-97d6-60332a952f6f" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503674 1074 "Mary and Sam meet when they are at the same distance from home. At this moment, their graphs intersect.\n\n![](images/8a/8afd4e9a8374ebe1e35200f6172e7fac9ef6c132d84e532c3ac44c25cb838a0d.png)\n\nIn this case, we have the point $(11,300).$ So, Mary and her brother met $11$ minutes since they started moving at a distance of $300\\,\\text{m}$ away from their home." 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503675 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503574 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503675 1069 "q-109042" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503675 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503675 1071 "Calculate $\\text{Var}[\\sqrt{b}]$ where $b\\ge 0$ is a constant." 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503675 1072 #uuid "359cefbe-6818-5849-92c9-d932909be650" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503675 1074 "Recall that for any constant $a,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[a]=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $b$ is a constant, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[\\sqrt{b}]=0.\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503676 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503548 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503676 1069 "q-32893" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503676 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503676 1071 "Given the curve $r(t)=t\\,i+(16+{t}^{3})\\,j$, find all points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel." 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503676 1072 #uuid "3a122c89-2594-51a0-a191-07ea14a78a1b" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503676 1074 "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(16+{t}^{3})\\,j \\\\  & =\\,i+3{t}^{2}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that $r({t}_{0})=c\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =c\\,{r}^{′}(t) \\\\ t\\,i+(16+{t}^{3})\\,j & =c(i+3{t}^{2}\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $i,$ and $j$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}t=c \\\\ 16+{t}^{3}=c(3{t}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation we obtain $t=c.$ Substituting $c=t$ into the second equality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16+{t}^{3} & =3{t}^{3} \\\\ 16 & =2{t}^{3} \\\\ t & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $r$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=2.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2) & =2\\,i+(16+{2}^{3})\\,j \\\\  & =2\\,i+24\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $P(2,24).$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503677 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503569 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503677 1069 "q-149457" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503677 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503677 1071 "The table below gives the values of a positive, continuous function $f(x)$ and its derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ over the closed interval $[1,9].$\nUsing a right Riemann sum with $4$ equal subintervals, estimate the arc length of the curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=1$ and $x=9.$\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $4$ | $5$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\sqrt{2}$ | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $4\\sqrt{5}$ | $0$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $0$ | $3\\sqrt{7}$ |\n\n*Hint: The arc length of a smooth curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ is given by*\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503677 1072 #uuid "3d6689df-3f70-5bf3-89ff-1a1c94f97ed3" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503677 1074 "Using the hint, we have that the arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=1$ and $x=9$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $g(x)=\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}.$ The (regular) step size $Δx$ is given by \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{9-1}{4}=2.\n$$\n\nWe can approximate the integral above using a *right* Riemann sum with $4$ equal subintervals and step size $Δx$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{9}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x≈(g(3)+g(5)+g(7)+g(9))\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nNow, we set up a table with values of $g(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $4\\sqrt{5}$ | $0$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $0$ | $3\\sqrt{7}$ |\n| $g(x)=\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}$ | $9$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $8$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our arc length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{9}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ≈(g(3)+g(5)+g(7)+g(9))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(1+2+1+8)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =12\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503678 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503584 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503678 1069 "q-225055" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503678 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503678 1071 "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X∼\\text{Po}(5)$ given below, compute $P(2\\le X\\le 4).$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\le x)$ | $\\,0.0067\\,$ | $\\,0.0404\\,$ | $\\,0.1247\\,$ | $\\,0.2650\\,$ | $\\,0.4405\\,$ | $\\,0.6160\\,$ | $\\,0.7622\\,$ |\n\n$P(2\\le X\\le 4)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503678 1072 #uuid "3df054eb-2d42-57cd-ac48-5335659c80b9" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503678 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(4) & =P(X\\le 4) \\\\  & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,2,3,4\\}) \\\\  & =P(X\\in \\{0,1\\})+P(X\\in \\{2,3,4\\}) \\\\  & =P(X\\le 1)+P(2\\le X\\le 4) \\\\  & =F(1)+P(2\\le X\\le 4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2\\le X\\le 4) & =F(4)-F(1) \\\\  & =0.4405-0.0404 \\\\  & =0.4001.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503679 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503564 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503679 1069 "q-165043" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503679 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503679 1071 "Given the curve $r(t)=({t}^{2}-2t+2)\\,i+(t-1)\\,j$, find all points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel." 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503679 1072 #uuid "400be170-ade2-5e44-a6d0-defdf287f3dd" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503679 1074 "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2t+2)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-1)\\,j \\\\  & =(2t-2)\\,i+\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that $r({t}_{0})=c\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =c\\,{r}^{′}(t) \\\\ ({t}^{2}-2t+2)\\,i+(t-1)\\,j & =c((2t-2)i+\\,j) \\\\ ({t}^{2}-2t+2)\\,i+(t-1)\\,j & =c(2t-2)i+c\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $i$ and $j$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}-2t+2=c(2t-2) \\\\ t-1=c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we obtain $t-1=c.$ Substituting $c=t-1$ into the first equality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}-2t+2 & =(t-1)(2t-2) \\\\ {t}^{2}-2t+2 & =2{t}^{2}-4t+2 \\\\ {t}^{2}-2t & =0 \\\\ t(t-2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $r$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel at the points corresponding to $t=0$ and $t=2.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =({0}^{2}-2(0)+2)\\,i+(0-1)\\,j=2\\,i-\\,j, \\\\ r(2) & =({2}^{2}-2(2)+2)\\,i+(2-1)\\,j=2\\,i+\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the points $(2,-1)$ and $(2,1).$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503680 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503640 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503680 1069 "q-44304" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503680 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503680 1071 "Calculate the $x$-intercept of the line $y=-2x+10.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503680 1072 #uuid "4459c635-09f2-5d35-8ba8-5eb9bfaa5126" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503680 1074 "At the $x$-intercept, the $y$-coordinate is equal to $0.$ So, we can substitute $y=0$ into the equation and then solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2x+10 \\\\ 0 & =-2x+10 \\\\ 2x & =10 \\\\ x & =\\frac{10}{2} \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercept is $x=5.$ The coordinates of the $x$-intercept are $(5,0).$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503681 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503595 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503681 1069 "q-216842" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503681 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503681 1071 "| $x$ | $-4.5$ | $-3.5$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $1.5$ | $2$ | $4.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3$ | $3$ | $0$ | $4$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n\nThe root of the function $f(x)$ is $x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503681 1072 #uuid "4a7f1341-51d1-5495-a7b0-9a9569fe4c9a" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503681 1074 "The roots of the function are the values of $x$ such that $f(x)=0.$ In the given table, we see that $f(-2)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the root of the function is $x=-2.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503682 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503517 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503682 1069 "q-357039" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503682 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503682 1071 "If $∠C$ and $∠D$ are complementary angles and $m∠C={51}^{∘}$, find the measure of $∠D.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503682 1072 #uuid "4b2d722a-94fe-5e1c-9243-0a6e73122040" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503682 1074 "![](images/a9/a910150e8bcfe1d8b4d5e16dd2244a4f5e4da7d6ad965b606293128f8595fa09.png)\n\nSince $∠C$ and $∠D$ are complementary, the sum of their measures is equal to ${90}^{∘}.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠C+m∠D & ={90}^{∘} \\\\ {51}^{∘}+m∠D & ={90}^{∘} \\\\ m∠D & ={39}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503683 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503530 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503683 1069 "q-118923" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503683 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503683 1071 "Consider a right triangle inscribed in a semicircle of radius\n$6.$ If the hypotenuse of the triangle lies along the diameter of the semicircle, what is the greatest possible perimeter of the triangle?" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503683 1072 #uuid "4ce61c51-3f39-5f7e-96d5-a322aa4a1799" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503683 1074 "We first draw a picture of the situation:\n\n![](images/41/417b284c87f735eb0c33f2369262b1548f9c54ff27ecbeb7880e91e9bc847f31.png)\n\nLet's call $l$ the length of one of the legs. By the Pythagorean theorem, the length of the other leg is $\\sqrt{144-{l}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(l) & =12+l+\\sqrt{144-{l}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $l$ is the length of a leg of a right triangle whose hypotenuse is $12,$ the permitted range of $l$ is $0<l<12.$\n\nWe want to maximize $P(l),$ so we need to compute the critical points. Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(l) & =1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{-2l}{\\sqrt{144-{l}^{2}}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{144-{l}^{2}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(l)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(l) & =0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{144-{l}^{2}}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{l}{\\sqrt{144-{l}^{2}}} & =1 \\\\ \\sqrt{144-{l}^{2}} & =l \\\\ 144-{l}^{2} & ={l}^{2} \\\\ 2{l}^{2} & =144 \\\\ {l}^{2} & =72 \\\\ l & =6\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $l=6\\sqrt{2}$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(l).$ To do this, we use the first derivative test. We write up a table of values:\n\n| Interval | $(0,6\\sqrt{2})$ | $6\\sqrt{2}$ | $(6\\sqrt{2},12)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ | $$ | $9$ |\n| ${P}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $P$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nSo, $l=6\\sqrt{2}$ does indeed give a maximum, and the perimeter of the resulting triangle will be the largest possible.\n\nTherefore, the greatest perimeter that a triangle can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(6\\sqrt{2}) & =12+6\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{144-(6\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =12+6\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{144-72} \\\\  & =12+6\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{72} \\\\  & =12+6\\sqrt{2}+6\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =12+12\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =12(1+\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503684 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503541 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503684 1069 "q-67416" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503684 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503684 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{2}{x-2}=4.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503684 1072 #uuid "528cc092-636b-5bd7-9846-07908de9113d" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503684 1074 "First, we note that $x=2$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the multiplication and the addition principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2)\\cdot (\\frac{x-2}{2}) & =2\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\ (\\cancel{2})\\cdot (\\frac{x-2}{\\cancel{2}}) & =\\frac{2}{4} \\\\ x-2 & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x-2+2 & =\\frac{1}{2}+2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503685 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503519 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503685 1069 "q-138073" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503685 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503685 1071 "Find the  maximum value of $Q(x)=-{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503685 1072 #uuid "5347ea8e-b936-5670-b92f-f003075bcc63" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503685 1074 "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )((-1-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-4) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -6) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +2) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,1,\\,-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}=3.\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503686 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503592 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503686 1069 "q-162932" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503686 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503686 1071 "$\\frac{\\sqrt{12{a}^{4}}}{\\sqrt{3}}=$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503686 1072 #uuid "53830319-4c61-59d6-b464-6d52b63f8b1c" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503686 1074 "First, we bring the quotient under a single radical and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\sqrt{12{a}^{4}}}{\\sqrt{3}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{12{a}^{4}}{3}}=\\sqrt{4{a}^{4}}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the expression inside the radical is a perfect square. So, we can continue simplifying by writing the expression as this perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{4{a}^{4}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(2{a}^{2})^{2}} & = \\\\ |2{a}^{2}| & = \\\\ |2|\\cdot |{a}^{2}| & = \\\\ 2\\cdot |{a}^{2}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA squared number is always positive or zero, and the absolute value of any positive (or zero) number is itself, so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot |{a}^{2}| & = \\\\ 2\\cdot {a}^{2} & = \\\\ 2{a}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503687 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503612 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503687 1069 "q-118710" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503687 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503687 1071 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{x}{x-1})}^{3x-3}.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503687 1072 #uuid "55d1a1bd-c8eb-5ee7-88d6-a44ae7dc2c96" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503687 1074 "Notice that as $x\\to \\infty ,$ \n\n- the expression inside the parentheses approaches $1,$ while\n\n- the exponent approaches $\\infty .$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{x}{x-1})}^{3x-3}={1}^{\\infty },\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}=e\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{x}{x-1})}^{3x-3} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{(x-1)+1}{x-1})}^{3x-3} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{x-1})}^{3(x-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $x-1=n.$ Therefore, since $n\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{x}{x-1})}^{3x-3} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{x-1})}^{3(x-1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{3n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{3} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{3} \\\\  & ={e}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503688 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503596 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503688 1069 "q-125343" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503688 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503688 1071 "$\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(\\sqrt{5})=$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503688 1072 #uuid "568ae2e9-d676-5003-b917-02b31b4feb79" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503688 1074 "The definition of $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(\\sqrt{5}) & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{\\sqrt{5}}+{e}^{-\\sqrt{5}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503689 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503594 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503689 1069 "q-342761" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503689 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503689 1071 "Evaluate $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\sqrt{2}).$ **Do not round** your answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\sqrt{2})=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\,-$ {{field-1}} $)$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503689 1072 #uuid "57b7ec72-0bfc-579f-8690-12d54f8319d9" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503689 1074 "The definition of $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\sqrt{2}) & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{\\sqrt{2}}-{e}^{-\\sqrt{2}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503690 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503606 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503690 1069 "q-98299" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503690 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503690 1071 "Which of the following curves is simple?" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503690 1072 #uuid "59e59f90-35db-5fb8-beec-b86dd49456dc" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503690 1074 "A simple curve is a curve that doesn’t intersect itself anywhere between its endpoints.\n\nAmong the given options, the only one that satisfies the definition is the following:\n\n![](images/59/59d3f7eac88247a8c2e1fcf4d0e09b2c28d579b23500ff8e620b4c3dec91ea39.png)" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503691 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503634 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503691 1069 "q-166756" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503691 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503691 1071 "The road between Mary's home and the community park is straight. What is the distance between Mary's home and the point halfway between Mary's home and the community park?\n\nThe distance between Mary's home and the halfway point is$\\,$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$meters." 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503691 1072 #uuid "5a6b0d11-b6de-598d-ac31-b89f5088f71e" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503691 1074 "![](images/d0/d06fbf44c15301b9fc764823eb6d51652d9fe9426b5aa01b764e4b409d9dc6d5.png)\n\n \n\nLet $H$ be Mary's home (located at $x=0\\,\\text{m}$) and let $P$ be the community park (located at $x=960\\,\\text{m}.$)\n\nTo find the number ${x}_{m}$ corresponding to the midpoint $M$ between Mary's home and the community park, we use the midpoint formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{m} & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+960}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{960}{2} \\\\  & =480.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/8b/8bba4fb81dc0a1e303a1f2bbe5e9d02fe159dfac83f29153401a2e7fda0b8df0.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the point that lies halfway between Mary's home and the community park is $480\\,\\text{m}$ from Mary's house." 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503692 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503622 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503692 1069 "q-166759" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503692 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503692 1071 "Using the data from part $5,$ find an **exact** equation of the line for the distance $d$ covered by Sam after $t$ minutes.\n\n$d=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503692 1072 #uuid "5d896b5e-5f76-50d5-9895-9e0c9b778976" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503692 1074 "We will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form $d=mt+b.$ So we need to work out the slope $m$ and the $d$-intercept $b.$\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the line intercepts the $d$-axis at the origin. So, the $d$-intercept is $b=0.$\n\nWe can calculate the slope of the line by choosing two points on the line and applying the slope formula. The line passes through the points $(0,0)$ and $(11,300),$ so we calculate its slope as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{d}_{2}-{d}_{1}}{{t}_{2}-{t}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{300-0}{11-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{300}{11}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the slope $m=\\frac{300}{11}$ and the $d$-intercept $b=0$ into the slope-intercept form $d=mt+b,$ we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{300}{11}t\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503693 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503572 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503693 1069 "q-225087" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503693 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503693 1071 "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X∼\\text{Po}(3.6)$ given below, compute $P(X\\ge 5).$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ | $8$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\le x)$ | $\\,0.5152\\,$ | $\\,0.7064\\,$ | $\\,0.8441\\,$ | $\\,0.9267\\,$ | $\\,0.9692\\,$ | $\\,0.9883\\,$ | $\\,0.9960\\,$ |\n\n$P(X\\ge 5)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503693 1072 #uuid "5f03e36d-2709-59cb-981a-892881905e64" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503693 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nTo solve the given problem, we can use the rule\n\n$$\nP(X>a)=1-P(X\\le a)=1-F(a).\n$$\n\nNow, note that \n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 5)=P(X>4).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 5) & =P(X>4) \\\\  & =1-P(X\\le 4) \\\\  & =1-F(4) \\\\  & =1-0.7064 \\\\  & =0.2936.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503694 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503615 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503694 1069 "q-5922" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503694 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503694 1071 "![](images/c4/c4ff51ad6db174c8bc6284a247f9ac6460de8b1483c211acaf68c5e8559bfaa5.png)\n\nSolve for $x$ given the figure above." 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503694 1072 #uuid "63e22990-215d-5b0e-8964-b23a81f16703" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503694 1074 "From the diagram, we have two complementary angles, so\n\n$$\n{54}^{∘}+(10x+{6}^{∘})={90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nHence, we can solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{54}^{∘}+(10x+{6}^{∘}) & ={90}^{∘} \\\\ 10x+{60}^{∘} & ={90}^{∘} \\\\ 10x & ={30}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={3}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503695 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503533 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503695 1069 "q-158455" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503695 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503695 1071 "Compute $P(-1<X<1)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{28}({x}^{2}+1),\\, & -2\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503695 1072 #uuid "6487d2c0-d995-5efa-98e2-2110fb813c4e" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503695 1074 "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1<X<1) & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{3}{28}({x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{28}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{28}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+x]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{28}([\\frac{1}{3}(1)^{3}+(1)]-[\\frac{1}{3}(-1)^{3}+(-1)]) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{28}([\\frac{1}{3}+1]-[-\\frac{1}{3}-1]) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{28}\\cdot \\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503696 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503562 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503696 1069 "q-216819" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503696 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503696 1071 "![](images/37/37439fb84da05737f6edb9f89b39171692fef6101b1f533ded725e62564d4670.png)\n\nThe root of the function shown above is$\\,x=$\n{{field-1}}\n." 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503696 1072 #uuid "6597c4d9-0b32-5959-9350-add218849a8e" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503696 1074 "The roots of the function are the $x$-intercepts. Therefore, the root is $x=-2.$\n\n![](images/b5/b5360593c2f639b831a19a97eb17d85199c5bf57a9d496739fa46a3a7e48ddd5.png)" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503697 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503599 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503697 1069 "q-237400" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503697 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503697 1071 "![](images/79/795f457314076b565d76a6b2579f2e1f87a247053dd0bb5de72225d63e17bf7f.png)\n\nGiven that $\\overset{―}{CE}$ is a segment, what is the measure of $∠COD?$\n\n$m∠COD=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503697 1072 #uuid "6af619db-0f0f-58ae-bb63-22eb9d582800" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503697 1074 "![](images/70/7045c84435a9ed2934569aceabf9d01e01d4278de6b89329664b654dc53b5250.png)\n\nTwo angles are supplementary if their measures add up to ${180}^{∘},$ a straight angle.\n\nSince $\\overset{―}{CE}$ is a segment, we have that $∠COD$ and $∠DOE$ are supplementary. \n\n$$\nm∠COD+m∠DOE={180}^{∘}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the measure of $∠COD$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠COD & ={180}^{∘}-m∠DOE \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{144}^{∘} \\\\  & ={36}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503698 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503625 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503698 1069 "q-82680" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503698 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503698 1071 "What is the root of the function $f(x)=1-4x?$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503698 1072 #uuid "6b18fe87-24b2-577e-8e31-abad021a59b4" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503698 1074 "The roots are the solutions to the equation $f(x)=0,$ which we solve as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =0 \\\\ 1-4x & =0 \\\\ 4x & =1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has the single root $x=\\frac{1}{4}.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503699 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503581 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503699 1069 "q-75204" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503699 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503699 1071 "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨2t-{t}^{2}\\,,\\,2{t}^{2}\\,,\\,2t+{t}^{2}⟩,$ find the point at which the tangent to the curve is parallel to the vector $⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,1⟩.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503699 1072 #uuid "6fb5b9b2-b9ce-5495-8ebc-1c2e3903a753" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503699 1074 "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2-2t,\\,4t,\\,2+2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,1⟩$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})=c\\,⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,1⟩.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =c\\,⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ⟨2-2t,\\,4t,\\,2+2t⟩ & =c\\,⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ⟨2-2t,\\,4t,\\,2+2t⟩ & =⟨0\\,,\\,c\\,,\\,c⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2-2t=0 \\\\ 4t=c \\\\ 2+2t=c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain that $t=1.$ Substituting to the second equation, we get $c=4.$\nThese values also satisfy the third equation. \n\nTherefore, $⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨2-{1}^{2}\\,,\\,2\\cdot {1}^{2}\\,,\\,2+{1}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2,\\,3⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $(1,2,3).$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503700 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503547 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503700 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503552 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503700 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503602 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503700 1069 "q-337372" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503700 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503700 1071 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y={e}^{-5x}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503700 1072 #uuid "74b05c82-78aa-5425-a974-d8adb06f503c" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503700 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{-2x})}^{′}+2(u{e}^{-2x}) & ={e}^{-5x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-2x}-2u{e}^{-2x}+2u{e}^{-2x} & ={e}^{-5x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-2x}-\\cancel{2u{e}^{-2x}}+\\cancel{2u{e}^{-2x}} & ={e}^{-5x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & ={e}^{-5x} \\\\ {u}^{′} & ={e}^{-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & ={e}^{-3x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{-2x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{-2x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3x}\\cdot {e}^{-2x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503701 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503632 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503701 1069 "q-141931" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503701 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503701 1071 "$\\sqrt[3]{2A}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{5B}=$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503701 1072 #uuid "750745d7-1de9-5ab2-9a88-105542b1b50e" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503701 1074 "We apply the product rule for radicals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{2A}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{5B} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{2A\\cdot 5B} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{10AB}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503702 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503557 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503702 1069 "q-237318" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503702 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503702 1071 "![](images/63/633e75b1b02b1eba8f512af09a88d7e671da2b238828529ace0a216688f48dd0.png)\n\nGiven that $\\overset{―}{AD}$ is a segment, what is the measure of $∠BOD?$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503702 1072 #uuid "7700589c-cccf-5be5-845d-a9a5e630ecf2" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503702 1074 "Two angles are supplementary if their measures add up to ${180}^{∘},$ a straight angle.\n\nSince $\\overset{―}{AD}$ is a segment, we have that $∠BOA$ and $∠BOD$ are supplementary. \n\n$$\nm∠BOA+m∠BOD={180}^{∘}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the measure of $∠BOD$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠BOD & ={180}^{∘}-m∠BOA \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{132}^{∘} \\\\  & ={48}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503703 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503563 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503703 1069 "q-141496" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503703 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503703 1071 "$\\sqrt{7w}\\cdot \\sqrt{5{z}^{3}}=$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503703 1072 #uuid "7a5ad26b-eab5-5fd9-938d-c3f9865b2926" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503703 1074 "We apply the product rule for radicals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{7w}\\cdot \\sqrt{5{z}^{3}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{7w\\cdot 5{z}^{3}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{35w{z}^{3}} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503704 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503605 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503704 1069 "q-224997" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503704 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503704 1071 "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X∼\\text{Po}(8)$ given below, compute $P(X<9).$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ | $8$ | $9$ | $10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\le x)$ | $\\,0.0996\\,$ | $\\,0.1912\\,$ | $\\,0.3134\\,$ | $\\,0.4530\\,$ | $\\,0.5925\\,$ | $\\,0.7166\\,$ | $\\,0.8159\\,$ |" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503704 1072 #uuid "7c63f903-029e-5df7-9333-d7481c32658b" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503704 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<9) & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,…,8\\}) \\\\  & =P(X\\le 8) \\\\  & =F(8) \\\\  & =0.5925.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503705 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503644 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503705 1069 "q-337257" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503705 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503705 1071 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-4y=5x-2.\n$$\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, which of the following is a suitable ansatz for the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)?$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503705 1072 #uuid "7dd414c2-6c98-512b-8e09-cef5e3ee65c4" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503705 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude." 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503706 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503571 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503706 1069 "q-106354" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503706 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503706 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{1}{x+5}=\\frac{3}{2x+12}.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503706 1072 #uuid "7e3eaae0-125c-585a-90f2-bbc8b4da35cd" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503706 1074 "First, we note that\n\n- $x=-5$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero\n\n- $x=-6$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+5}{1}=\\frac{2x+12}{3}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator, $3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x+15}{3}=\\frac{2x+12}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot (\\frac{3x+15}{3}) & =3\\cdot (\\frac{2x+12}{3}) \\\\ 3x+15 & =2x+12 \\\\ 3x-2x & =12-15 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503707 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503568 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503707 1069 "q-108938" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503707 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503707 1071 "If $\\text{Var}[X]=6,$ calculate $\\text{Var}[-3X].$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503707 1072 #uuid "8094ceca-7731-54ab-bb63-d28e40db7d50" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503707 1074 "Recall that for any random variable $X$ and constant $a,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX]={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X].\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=-3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[-3X] & =(-3)^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =9\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =54.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503708 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503588 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503708 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503600 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503708 1069 "q-212289" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503708 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503708 1071 "![](images/f8/f822ed2c39270fde5dfea6fa50c6985a837b45160c6f5366636d04c5908c5790.png)\n\nWhat are the roots of the function $f(x)$ defined by the mapping diagram shown above?\n\nIn **ascending order**, the roots are $x=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503708 1072 #uuid "80fe3e8e-f7e1-5a2a-9e77-9f545ee857e4" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503708 1074 "The roots of the function are the values of $x$ such that $f(x)=0.$ In the given mapping diagram, we see that $f(-2)=0$ and $f(-1)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the roots of the function are $x=-2$ and $x=-1.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503709 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503553 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503709 1069 "q-95329" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503709 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503709 1071 "$$\n\nFind the derivative of $g(x)=\\frac{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}{2x+1}.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503709 1072 #uuid "828244f0-37d1-5218-b4cd-65ea8c882592" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503709 1074 "In this case, the expression for $g(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)={sinh}^{2}⁡x$ and $v(x)=2x+1.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({sinh}^{2}⁡x)^{′}\\cdot (2x+1)-{sinh}^{2}⁡x\\cdot (2x+1)^{′}}{(2x+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)\\cdot (2x+1)-{sinh}^{2}⁡x\\cdot (2)}{(2x+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(2x+1)\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-2{sinh}^{2}⁡x}{(2x+1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503710 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503526 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503710 1069 "q-357050" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503710 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503710 1071 "If $∠X$ and $∠Y$ are complementary angles and $m∠X={47}^{∘}$, then $m∠Y=${{field-1}}\n${}^{∘}.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503710 1072 #uuid "828e982d-a9a1-585f-8390-ab20f7e96140" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503710 1074 "![](images/80/8079f80c1adad20a0844885f06cb56feb8fc55d8172aa154d4d2c484c3b977cc.png)\n\nSince $∠X$ and $∠Y$ are complementary, the sum of their measures is equal to ${90}^{∘}.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠X+m∠Y & ={90}^{∘} \\\\ {47}^{∘}+m∠Y & ={90}^{∘} \\\\ m∠Y & ={43}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503711 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503611 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503711 1069 "q-48021" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503711 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503711 1071 "Approximate the value of $\\sqrt{37}$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ at $x=36.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503711 1072 #uuid "82a6bab7-1298-5d9a-9389-41a0c015118d" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503711 1074 "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $x=a$ is\n\n$$\nL(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=36,$ which gives\n\n$$\nL(x)=f(36)+{f}^{′}(36)(x-36).\n$$\n\nLet's compute $f(36)$ and ${f}^{′}(36)$ in the expression above. Evaluating the function, we get\n\n$$\nf(36)=\\sqrt{36}=6,\n$$\n\nand computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x})=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(36)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{36}}=\\frac{1}{12}.\n$$\n\nSo, the tangent line approximation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x)=6+\\frac{1}{12}(x-36).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $\\sqrt{37}$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=37.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{37} & ≈L(37) \\\\  & =6+\\frac{1}{12}(37-36) \\\\  & =6+\\frac{1}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{73}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503712 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503549 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503712 1069 "q-138072" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503712 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503712 1071 "Find the minimum value of $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{3}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503712 1072 #uuid "8706edb2-e78b-5b3f-a44f-7250c01b8006" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503712 1074 "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-4) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,1,\\,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}=-1.\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503713 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503536 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503713 1069 "q-116957" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503713 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503713 1071 "![](images/53/53cb737e649dea47bb90bf8e365d52448156609d92004bc187f552d5bb0cf62f.png)\n\nSolve for $x$ given the figure above." 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503713 1072 #uuid "89999399-d3ff-5fdc-bb8f-b862f007ad4a" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503713 1074 "Since the two angles are supplementary, the sum of their measures is equal to ${180}^{∘}.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+4x-{9}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 7x-{9}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 7x & ={189}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={27}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503714 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503614 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503714 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503619 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503714 1069 "q-216848" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503714 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503714 1071 "![](images/ac/acc14b61036a00f4fae8d850954293c99d48388a8b4a83dda158bf1688d97c62.png)\n\nWhat are the roots of the function $f(x)$ defined by the mapping diagram shown above?\n\nIn **ascending order**, the roots are $x=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503714 1072 #uuid "8e742ff0-460d-5812-916a-b9b820b7f39f" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503714 1074 "The roots of the function are the values of $x$ such that $f(x)=0.$ In the given mapping diagram, we see that $f(3)=0$ and $f(4)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the roots of the function are $x=3$ and $x=4.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503715 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503621 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503715 1069 "q-67407" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503715 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503715 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{4}{5}=\\frac{4}{3x}.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503715 1072 #uuid "9423a212-cd5b-5be8-a581-5c447b673232" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503715 1074 "First, we note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{4}=\\frac{3x}{4}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4} & =\\frac{3x}{4} \\\\ 4\\cdot \\frac{5}{4} & =4\\cdot \\frac{3x}{4} \\\\ \\cancel{4}\\cdot \\frac{5}{\\cancel{4}} & =\\cancel{4}\\cdot \\frac{3x}{\\cancel{4}} \\\\ 5 & =3x \\\\ (\\frac{1}{3})\\cdot 5 & =(\\frac{1}{3})\\cdot (3x) \\\\ \\frac{5}{3} & =\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3}}\\cdot (\\cancel{3}x) \\\\ \\frac{5}{3} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the solution is $x=\\frac{5}{3}.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503716 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503515 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503716 1069 "q-224995" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503716 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503716 1071 "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X∼\\text{Po}(9)$ given below, find the value of $P(X\\le 7).$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ | $8$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\le x)$ | $\\,0.0212\\,$ | $\\,0.0550\\,$ | $\\,0.1157\\,$ | $\\,0.2068\\,$ | $\\,0.3239\\,$ | $\\,0.4557\\,$ | $\\,0.5874\\,$ |" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503716 1072 #uuid "951aecd2-9a03-5921-a647-8badf487101e" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503716 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 7) & =F(7) \\\\  & =0.3239.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503717 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503628 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503717 1069 "q-342677" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503717 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503717 1071 "Simplify $tanh\\,(\\frac{k}{3})$ in terms of $e$ using the definition of the hyperbolic tangent.\n\n$tanh\\,(\\frac{k}{3})=$\n\n| {{field-1}} |\n| --- |\n| ${e}^{2k/3}+1$ |" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503717 1072 #uuid "957cf846-05af-527e-b9b5-8a808e2c175e" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503717 1074 "The definition of $\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x & =\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}-1}{{e}^{2x}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-2x}}{1+{e}^{-2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the second definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tanh\\,(\\frac{k}{3}) & =\\frac{{e}^{2(k/3)}-1}{{e}^{2(k/3)}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2k/3}-1}{{e}^{2k/3}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503718 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503531 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503718 1069 "q-95492" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503718 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503718 1071 "Given that $z>0,$ which of the following is equivalent to $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡z)?$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503718 1072 #uuid "958048ce-f9d1-5782-ad7f-1bcc0753619d" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503718 1074 "The definition of $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡z) & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}-{e}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{z}^{2}}-{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{z}^{-2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({z}^{2}-{z}^{-2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({z}^{2}-\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{{z}^{4}-1}{{z}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{z}^{4}-1}{2{z}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503719 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503544 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503719 1069 "q-300746" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503719 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503719 1071 "Express $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(-\\frac{3x}{4})$ in terms of $e$ using the definition of the hyperbolic cosine.\n\n$\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(-\\frac{3x}{4})=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503719 1072 #uuid "98232048-0528-5aa4-8a77-d211e3369a57" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503719 1074 "The definition of $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(-\\frac{3x}{4}) & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-3x/4}+{e}^{-(-3x/4)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-3x/4}+{e}^{3x/4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503720 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503576 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503720 1069 "q-6944" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503720 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503720 1071 "Determine the equation of the line that passes through the points $(4,7)$ and $(5,17).$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503720 1072 #uuid "9be101d3-9545-55c8-953e-3e73d5bfedaf" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503720 1074 "We will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$ So, we need to work out the slope $m$ and the $y$-intercept $b.$\n\nFirst, we calculate the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{17-7}{5-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{1} \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the slope $m=10$ into the slope-intercept formula $y=mx+b,$ we reach\n\n$$\ny=10x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can find this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(4,7):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =10x+b \\\\ 7 & =10(4)+b \\\\ 7 & =40+b \\\\ 7-40 & =b \\\\ -33 & =b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the line is $y=10x-33.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503721 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503623 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503721 1069 "q-162959" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503721 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503721 1071 "Simplify $\\sqrt{\\frac{36}{121{t}^{2}}}$ given that $t<0.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503721 1072 #uuid "a2aa05db-1a86-5603-983d-94f822e3df23" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503721 1074 "First, we put the numerator and denominator under separate radicals and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\frac{36}{121{t}^{2}}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{36}}{\\sqrt{121{t}^{2}}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{{6}^{2}}}{\\sqrt{121{t}^{2}}}=\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{121{t}^{2}}}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the expression inside the radical is a perfect square. So, we can continue simplifying by writing the expression as this perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{121{t}^{2}}} & = \\\\ \\frac{6}{\\sqrt{(11t)^{2}}} & = \\\\ \\frac{6}{|11t|} & = \\\\ \\frac{6}{|11|\\cdot |t|} & = \\\\ \\frac{6}{11\\cdot |t|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we are told that $t<0,$ so we have $|t|=-t.$ Therefore, the final answer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{11\\cdot |t|} & = \\\\ \\frac{6}{11\\cdot (-t)} & = \\\\ -\\frac{6}{11t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503722 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503518 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503722 1069 "q-166757" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503722 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503722 1071 "The equation that describes Mary's distance from home is $d=960-60t.$ Hence, Mary reaches the midpoint $M$ after$\\,${{field-1}}\n$\\,$ minutes." 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503722 1072 #uuid "a524e974-5d6b-572b-94df-d3f5d29063a3" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503722 1074 "Since the midpoint is located at $d=480$, we need to solve $960-60t=480.$\n\nFirst, we apply the addition principle, subtracting $960$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}960-60t & =480 \\\\ 960-60t-960 & =480-960 \\\\ -60t+0 & =-480 \\\\ -60t & =-480\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSecond, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $-60:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}-60t & =-480 \\\\ \\frac{-60t}{-60} & =\\frac{-480}{-60} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{-60}t}{\\cancel{-60}} & =8 \\\\ t & =8.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Mary reaches the halfway point after $8$ minutes." 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503723 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503532 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503723 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503587 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503723 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503603 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503723 1069 "q-337452" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503723 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503723 1071 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3y={e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503723 1072 #uuid "a6116c6c-0d2d-5d26-9e6b-b6dd0de87989" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503723 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{-3x})}^{′}+3(u{e}^{-3x}) & ={e}^{-3x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-3x}-3u{e}^{-3x}+3u{e}^{-3x} & ={e}^{-3x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-3x}-\\cancel{3u{e}^{-3x}}+\\cancel{3u{e}^{-3x}} & ={e}^{-3x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-3x} & ={e}^{-3x} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =1 \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{-3x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{-3x} \\\\  & =x{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503724 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503540 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503724 1069 "q-237390" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503724 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503724 1071 "![](images/8e/8e8f3df43e45dd6916a35c3e9a41fb517f94d32591f656628c5723fd4d0f754d.png)\n\nGiven that $\\overset{―}{CE}$ is a segment, what is the measure of $∠DOC?$\n\n$m∠DOC=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503724 1072 #uuid "a68d95ce-c563-5215-b1b0-c9bfcf7d73e2" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503724 1074 "![](images/4f/4f0f4b52fc5b84d2d63281da0bc8d92f7433a248550e81c27b0a92a6e5d58a34.png)\n\nTwo angles are supplementary if their measures add up to ${180}^{∘},$ a straight angle.\n\nSince $\\overset{―}{CE}$ is a segment, we have that $∠DOE$ and $∠DOC$ are supplementary. \n\n$$\nm∠DOE+m∠DOC={180}^{∘}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the measure of $∠DOC$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠DOC & ={180}^{∘}-m∠DOE \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{67}^{∘} \\\\  & ={113}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503725 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503639 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503725 1069 "q-164846" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503725 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503725 1071 "Given the curve $r(t)=2t\\,i+({t}^{2}-1)\\,j$, find all of the points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are perpendicular." 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503725 1072 #uuid "a768b90b-915c-5552-9320-dfc50699b25f" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503725 1074 "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-1)\\,j \\\\  & =2i+2t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ [2t\\,i+({t}^{2}-1)\\,j]\\cdot (2i+2t\\,j) & =0 \\\\ 2t\\cdot 2+({t}^{2}-1)\\cdot 2t & =0 \\\\ 2t(2+{t}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ 2t({t}^{2}+1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $t=0$ since ${t}^{2}+1>0$ for any $t.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0)=2(0)\\,i+((0)^{2}-1)\\,j=-j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n we conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(0,-1).$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503726 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503567 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503726 1069 "q-166755" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503726 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503726 1071 "Mary covered a distance of $960$ meters in $16$ minutes. Then, Mary's speed was{{field-1}} meters per minute." 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503726 1072 #uuid "a8749a36-d229-5422-be78-2c6cc7c08abc" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503726 1074 "Speed is a special unit rate applied to moving objects. It is defined as *the distance traveled per unit of time*:\n\n$$\n\\text{speed}=\\frac{\\text{distance}}{\\text{time}}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed in meters per minute, we divide the number of meters, $960,$ by the number of minutes, $16.$ This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{960}{16} & =\\frac{960÷8}{16÷8} \\\\  & =\\frac{120}{2} \\\\  & =120÷2 \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Mary's speed was $60$ meters per minute." 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503727 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503516 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503727 1069 "q-3564" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503727 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503727 1071 "Given that \n\n$$\n{(2{t}^{2}-3s)}^{4}=A{t}^{8}+B{t}^{6}s+C{t}^{4}{s}^{2}-216{t}^{2}{s}^{3}+81{s}^{4}\n$$\n\nwhere $A,B$ and $C$ are constants, determine the value of $A+B+C.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503727 1072 #uuid "ab532852-8985-56d4-9487-9412ea181516" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503727 1074 "First, let's compare our expression with the binomial\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}.\n$$\n\nHere we have that $n=4,$ $a=2{t}^{2},$ and $b=-3s.$ So, the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{a}^{4}{b}^{0},\\,{a}^{3}{b}^{1},\\,{a}^{2}{b}^{2},\\,{a}^{1}{b}^{3},\\,{a}^{0}{b}^{4}.\n$$\n\nThe $4$th row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,4,6,4,1).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2{t}^{2}-3s)^{4} & =1{a}^{4}+4{a}^{3}b+6{a}^{2}{b}^{2}+4a{b}^{3}+1{b}^{4} \\\\  & =1{(2{t}^{2})}^{4}(-3s)^{0}+4{(2{t}^{2})}^{3}(-3s)^{1}+6{(2{t}^{2})}^{2}(-3s)^{2} \\\\  & \\,+4{(2{t}^{2})}^{1}(-3s)^{3}+1{(2{t}^{2})}^{0}(-3s)^{4} \\\\  & =1(16{t}^{8})(1)+4(8{t}^{6})(-3s)+6(4{t}^{4})(9{s}^{2}) \\\\  & \\,+4(2{t}^{2})(-27{s}^{3})+1(1)(81{s}^{4}) \\\\  & =16{t}^{8}-96{t}^{6}s+216{t}^{4}{s}^{2}-216{t}^{2}{s}^{3}+81{s}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A=16,B=-96,$ and $C=216.$ Finally,\n\n$$\nA+B+C=16-96+216=136.\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503728 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503585 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503728 1069 "q-90726" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503728 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503728 1071 "Given that $y=y(x)$ satisfies the equation ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+8y=0,$ which of the following **cannot** be $y(x)?$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503728 1072 #uuid "ac3ed9ab-9e20-50c1-a2df-d0e7acffd405" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503728 1074 "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+6\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+8{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +8) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +8 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +2)(\\lambda +4)=0,\n$$\n\nand so the solutions of the auxiliary equation  are $\\lambda =-2$ or $\\lambda =-4.$\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{-2x}$ and ${e}^{-4x}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only ${e}^{-6x}$ is not a solution." 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503729 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503629 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503729 1069 "q-67422" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503729 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503729 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{6}{x+2}=\\frac{3}{x}.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503729 1072 #uuid "b01bcc83-5d54-554e-8eb4-318389e1d142" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503729 1074 "First, we note that\n\n- $x=-2$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero\n\n- $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+2}{6}=\\frac{x}{3}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator $6.$ The above equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+2}{6}=\\frac{2x}{6}.\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\cdot \\frac{x+2}{6} & =6\\cdot \\frac{2x}{6} \\\\ x+2 & =2x \\\\ 2 & =2x-x \\\\ 2 & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the solution is $x=2.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503730 1069 "e-18990" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503730 1071 "The angles $\\angle a$ and $\\angle b$ are complementary angles. If $m \\angle a = 37^\\circ,$ what is the measure of $\\angle b?$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503730 1072 #uuid "b2c15979-d071-5f8a-b3a3-0e1228124d71" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503730 1074 "![](images/18/18d67b5d6d05c29fdf286406cdb8f1615f623c942c55d414b0655c8ac0ef242b.png)\n\nSince $\\angle a$ and $\\angle b$ are complementary, the sum of their measures is equal to $90^\\circ.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠a+m∠b & ={90}^{∘} \\\\ {37}^{∘}+m∠b & ={90}^{∘} \\\\ m∠b & ={90}^{∘}-{37}^{∘} \\\\ m∠b & ={53}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503731 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503627 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503731 1069 "q-141846" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503731 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503731 1071 "$\\sqrt[3]{x}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{8{x}^{2}}=$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503731 1072 #uuid "b49efc81-0ddf-5a4e-8d55-47ad95f0b7d1" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503731 1074 "We perform the multiplication under a single radical and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{x}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{8{x}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{x\\cdot 8{x}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{8\\cdot {x}^{3}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{(2x)^{3}} & = \\\\ 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503732 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503520 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503732 1069 "q-342686" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503732 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503732 1071 "Express $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡5x$ in terms of $e$ using the definition of the hyperbolic sine.\n\n$\\operatorname{sinh}⁡5x=$\n {{field-1}}" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503732 1072 #uuid "b4db71bf-bb20-5cb8-bf0c-68dc6c798e64" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503732 1074 "The definition of $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡5x=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{5x}-{e}^{-5x}).\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503733 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503550 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503733 1069 "q-110957" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503733 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503733 1071 "If $z\\ge 0,$ then $\\sqrt{3z}\\cdot \\sqrt{5{z}^{6}}=$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503733 1072 #uuid "b57aff47-fce6-506a-bd4c-d85de1a296a5" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503733 1074 "We perform the multiplication under a single radical and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3z}\\cdot \\sqrt{5{z}^{6}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{3z\\cdot 5{z}^{6}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{{z}^{6}\\cdot 15z} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{{({z}^{3})}^{2}\\cdot 15z} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{{({z}^{3})}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{15z} & = \\\\ |{z}^{3}|\\cdot \\sqrt{15z} & = \\\\ |z{|}^{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{15z} & =\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $z\\ge 0,$ we have $|z|=z,$ and we can simplify further. Therefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n|z{|}^{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{15z}={z}^{3}\\sqrt{15z}.\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503734 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503529 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503734 1069 "q-357053" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503734 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503734 1071 "![](images/93/9306c9b7f6dfed945d955c27d12635bf5b1952288d5c833e22737d4c39da8ad2.png)\n\nSolve for $x$ given the figure above.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503734 1072 #uuid "b8987b97-dcba-5e61-82e1-c9e2d8c85a88" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503734 1074 "From the diagram, we have two complementary angles, so\n\n$$\n(x+{15}^{∘})+(4x-{5}^{∘})={90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nHence, we can solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+{15}^{∘})+(4x-{5}^{∘}) & ={90}^{∘} \\\\ 5x+{10}^{∘} & ={90}^{∘} \\\\ 5x & ={80}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={16}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503735 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503597 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503735 1069 "q-225067" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503735 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503735 1071 "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X∼\\text{Po}(4)$ given below, compute $P(3\\le X<6).$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\le x)$ | $\\,0.0916\\,$ | $\\,0.2381\\,$ | $\\,0.4335\\,$ | $\\,0.6288\\,$ | $\\,0.7851\\,$ | $\\,0.8893\\,$ | $\\,0.9489\\,$ |" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503735 1072 #uuid "b92762e3-576d-55cb-b009-a97e0be6338a" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503735 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(5) & =P(X<6) \\\\  & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,2,3,4,5\\}) \\\\  & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,2\\})+P(X\\in \\{3,4,5\\}) \\\\  & =P(X\\le 2)+P(3\\le X<6) \\\\  & =F(2)+P(3\\le X<6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3\\le X<6) & =F(5)-F(2) \\\\  & =0.7851-0.2381 \\\\  & =0.5470.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503736 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503618 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503736 1069 "q-357055" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503736 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503736 1071 "![](images/d3/d3356cf5182c27467cc122249c4212b81934825bfcb96d4f62489d485218a973.png)\n\nSolve for $x$ given the figure above.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503736 1072 #uuid "bc96f5c1-f42e-58eb-b44f-349743640f2a" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503736 1074 "From the diagram, we have two complementary angles, so\n\n$$\n(2x+{14}^{∘})+(4x-{8}^{∘})={90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nHence, we can solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x+{14}^{∘})+(4x-{8}^{∘}) & ={90}^{∘} \\\\ 6x+{6}^{∘} & ={90}^{∘} \\\\ 6x & ={84}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={14}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503737 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503545 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503737 1069 "q-342759" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503737 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503737 1071 "Evaluate $sinh\\,(\\frac{3}{2}).$ **Do not round** your answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{3/2}\\,-$ {{field-1}} $)$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503737 1072 #uuid "be80c98f-2a34-525c-9b0c-3079917fdbf6" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503737 1074 "The definition of $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sinh\\,(\\frac{3}{2}) & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{3/2}-{e}^{-3/2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503738 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503610 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503738 1069 "q-237280" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503738 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503738 1071 "![](images/3f/3f8a1a74aa893bfd77d55fdd718c28289919eb42eda74040aa7b57fa86c9af46.png)\n\nWhat angle is supplementary to $∠EOA?$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503738 1072 #uuid "be8b75c2-d40f-5057-b05b-7babfebbf644" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503738 1074 "![](images/6c/6cd7abdc64a8ee9a9ae1a5ffacf59d7d68673bb338a1a1a9b41f4e2457a7588e.png)\n\nTwo angles are supplementary if their measures add up to ${180}^{∘},$ a straight angle.\n\nOnly $∠AOC$ is supplementary to $∠EOA$ in the given diagram." 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503739 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503633 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503739 1069 "q-32919" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503739 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503739 1071 "Find a vector that's tangent to the curve $r(t)=t\\,i+2\\sqrt{t}\\,j+\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+3}\\,k$ at the point $P(1,2,2).$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503739 1072 #uuid "befac87f-c42c-5c4b-8db1-52723d372dd2" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503739 1074 "The point $P(1,2,2)$ is at the tip of $r(t)$ for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t.$ At this point, the vector ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to $P.$ We know that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =i+2\\,j+2\\,k \\\\ t\\,i+2\\sqrt{t}\\,j+\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+3}\\,k & =i+2\\,j+2\\,k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand equating the coefficients of $i,j,$ and $k$ yields the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}t=1 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{t}=2 \\\\ \\sqrt{{t}^{2}+3}=2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $t=1.$ This value also satifies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(1,2,2)$ is at the tip of the vector $r(1).$  \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{t})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+3})\\,k \\\\  & =\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}\\,j+\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+3}}\\,k \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(1)}}\\,j+\\frac{(1)}{\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+3}}\\,k \\\\  & =\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the curve at the point $P(1,2,2)$ is $\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503740 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503579 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503740 1069 "q-35762" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503740 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503740 1071 "Find the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}-6}.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503740 1072 #uuid "c664e1c1-92ad-575b-bafc-9b1ddd2a888d" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503740 1074 "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}-6} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}})}{(1-\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$ is $y=0.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503741 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503583 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503741 1069 "q-12884" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503741 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503741 1071 "Find, in descending powers of $y$ and ascending powers of $x,$ the first three terms of the binomial expansion of ${(y-x)}^{3}.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503741 1072 #uuid "c989ddc2-999c-5079-ab1b-dc05c9512624" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503741 1074 "First, let's compare our expression with the binomial\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}.\n$$\n\nHere we have that $n=3,$ $a=y,$ and $b=-x.$ So, the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{a}^{3}{b}^{0},\\,{a}^{2}{b}^{1},\\,{a}^{1}{b}^{2},\\,{a}^{0}{b}^{3}.\n$$\n\nThe $3$rd row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,3,3,1).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(y-x)}^{3} & =1{a}^{3}+3{a}^{2}b+3a{b}^{2}+1{b}^{3} \\\\  & =1(y)^{3}(-x)^{0}+3(y)^{2}(-x)^{1}+3(y)^{1}(-x)^{2}+1(y)^{0}(-x)^{3} \\\\  & =1({y}^{3})(1)+3({y}^{2})(-x)+3(y)({x}^{2})+1(1)(-{x}^{3}) \\\\  & ={y}^{3}-3{y}^{2}x+3y{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in descending powers of $y$ and ascending powers of $x,$ the first three terms are\n\n$$\n{y}^{3}-3{y}^{2}x+3y{x}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503742 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503537 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503742 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503613 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503742 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503630 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503742 1069 "q-337398" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503742 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503742 1071 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y=3x+1.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503742 1072 #uuid "ca23d1f9-e0ba-5c4f-a4c8-1fb3f4fded3f" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503742 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{2x})}^{′}-2(u{e}^{2x}) & =3x+1 \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2u{e}^{2x}-2u{e}^{2x} & =3x+1 \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x}+\\cancel{2u{e}^{2x}}-\\cancel{2u{e}^{2x}} & =3x+1 \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x} & =3x+1 \\\\ {u}^{′} & ={e}^{-2x}(3x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & ={e}^{-2x}(3x+1) \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{-2x}(3x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =\\int {e}^{-2x}(3x+1)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{2x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{2x} \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{-2x}(3x+1)\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {e}^{2x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\int {e}^{-2x}(3x+1)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503743 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503645 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503743 1069 "q-141845" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503743 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503743 1071 "$\\sqrt{16w}\\cdot 3\\sqrt{9{w}^{3}}=$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503743 1072 #uuid "d17d0de9-c925-52f5-bd49-81ba61ecf89c" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503743 1074 "We perform the multiplication under a single radical and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{16w}\\cdot 3\\sqrt{9{w}^{3}} & = \\\\ 3\\sqrt{16w\\cdot 9{w}^{3}} & = \\\\ 3\\sqrt{144{w}^{4}} & = \\\\ 3\\sqrt{(12{w}^{2})^{2}} & = \\\\ 3|12{w}^{2}| & = \\\\ 3\\cdot |12|\\cdot |{w}^{2}| & = \\\\ 3\\cdot 12\\cdot |{w}^{2}| & = \\\\ 36\\cdot |{w}^{2}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA squared number is always positive or zero, and the absolute value of any positive (or zero) number is itself, so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36\\cdot |{w}^{2}| & =36{w}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503744 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503616 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503744 1069 "q-166754" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503744 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503744 1071 "The slope of the line is $m\\,=\\,${{field-1}}." 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503744 1072 #uuid "d59c0884-c058-5785-9ee6-42436f91acb4" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503744 1074 "Note that the line passes through the points $(0,960)$ and $(16,0).$ So, we let $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,960)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(16,0),$ and compute the slope using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-960}{16-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-960}{16} \\\\  & =-60\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the line is $m=-60.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503745 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503635 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503745 1069 "q-3563" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503745 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503745 1071 "Find, in ascending powers of $y,$ the first three terms of the binomial expansion of ${(10y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{5}.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503745 1072 #uuid "d6a9fa51-d96f-5308-9675-af7e9df7f105" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503745 1074 "First, let's compare our expression with the binomial\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}.\n$$\n\nHere we have that $n=5,$ $a=10y,$ and $b=\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{a}^{5}{b}^{0},\\,{a}^{4}{b}^{1},\\,{a}^{3}{b}^{2},\\,{a}^{2}{b}^{3},\\,{a}^{1}{b}^{4},\\,{a}^{0}{b}^{5}.\n$$\n\nThe $5$th row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,5,10,10,5,1).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(10y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{5} & =1{a}^{5}+5{a}^{4}b+10{a}^{3}{b}^{2}+10{a}^{2}{b}^{3}+5a{b}^{4}+1{b}^{5} \\\\  & =1(10y)^{5}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{0}+5(10y)^{4}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{1}+10(10y)^{3}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & \\,+10(10y)^{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}+5(10y)^{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4}+1(10y)^{0}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5} \\\\  & =1(100\\,000{y}^{5})(1)+5(10\\,000{y}^{4})(\\frac{1}{2})+10(1\\,000{y}^{3})(\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & \\,+10(100{y}^{2})(\\frac{1}{8})+5(10y)(\\frac{1}{16})+1(1)(\\frac{1}{32}) \\\\  & =100\\,000{y}^{5}+\\frac{50\\,000{y}^{4}}{2}+\\frac{10\\,000{y}^{3}}{4}+\\frac{1\\,000{y}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{50y}{16}+\\frac{1}{32} \\\\  & =100\\,000{y}^{5}+25\\,000{y}^{4}+2\\,500{y}^{3}+125{y}^{2}+\\frac{25y}{8}+\\frac{1}{32}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in ascending powers of $y$, the first three terms are\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{32}+\\frac{25y}{8}+125{y}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503746 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503573 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503746 1069 "q-98313" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503746 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503746 1071 "Which of the following curves is *not* simple?" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503746 1072 #uuid "da08f493-0f5f-5868-922d-213ef6fbf52a" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503746 1074 "A simple curve is a curve that doesn’t intersect itself anywhere between its endpoints.\n\nSo, a curve that intersects itself somewhere between its endpoints is not a simple curve. Among the given options, the only that is not a simple curve is the following:\n\n \n\n![](images/34/3441f522bfad1f9495f5dd94dab875d24b1916d0f2aaae9920995e8721e8b6c4.png)" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503747 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503566 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503747 1069 "q-165036" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503747 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503747 1071 "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3},3{t}^{2}+4t⟩,$ find the point at which the tangent to the curve is parallel to the vector $⟨1,-1⟩.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503747 1072 #uuid "da548a97-bf75-53d6-96e0-6ed458e117e2" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503747 1074 "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}+4t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{t}^{2},6t+4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,3⟩$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})=c⟨1,-1⟩.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =c⟨1,-1⟩ \\\\ ⟨2{t}^{2},6t+4⟩ & =c⟨1,-1⟩ \\\\ ⟨2{t}^{2},6t+4⟩ & =⟨c,-c⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2}=c \\\\ 6t+4=-c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain that $c=2{t}^{2}.$ Substituting $c=2{t}^{2}$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6t+4 & =-2{t}^{2} \\\\ 2{t}^{2}+6t+4 & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}+3t+2 & =0 \\\\ (t+2)(t+1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $⟨1,-1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel at the points corresponding to $t=-2$ and $t=-1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1) & =⟨\\frac{2}{3}(-1)^{3},3(-1)^{2}+4(-1)⟩=⟨-\\frac{2}{3},-1⟩, \\\\ r(-2) & =⟨\\frac{2}{3}(-2)^{3},3(-2)^{2}+4(-2)⟩=⟨-\\frac{16}{3},4⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨1,-1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the points $(-\\frac{2}{3},-1)$ and $(-\\frac{16}{3},4).$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503748 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503641 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503748 1069 "q-357054" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503748 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503748 1071 "![](images/49/490d1cc553867d48b934ad72c42fa69c355d7d8facf0999034fc6ca9e4fa7323.png)\n\nSolve for $x$ given the figure above.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503748 1072 #uuid "db49ef55-d719-58c2-91e1-31736abaa714" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503748 1074 "From the diagram, we have two complementary angles, so\n\n$$\n{28}^{∘}+(3x+{5}^{∘})={90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nHence, we can solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{28}^{∘}+(3x+{5}^{∘}) & ={90}^{∘} \\\\ 3x+{33}^{∘} & ={90}^{∘} \\\\ 3x & ={57}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={19}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503749 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503617 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503749 1069 "q-158531" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503749 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503749 1071 "Find the minimum value of $Q(x)=4{x}_{1}^{2}+7{x}_{2}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503749 1072 #uuid "df4de458-1c06-5aa0-8cdd-76f4342f8dfd" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503749 1074 "First, notice that ${x}_{2}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n7{x}_{2}^{2}\\ge 4{x}_{2}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =4{x}_{1}^{2}+7{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & \\ge 4{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =4({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2})=4(1)=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\ge 4.$ \n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no smaller than $4.$ But before we conclude that $4$ is the minimum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=4.$\n\nNotice that when  $x={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =4{x}_{1}^{2}+7{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =4(1)+7(0)^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the minimum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $4.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503750 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503522 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503750 1069 "q-339604" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503750 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503750 1071 "Evaluate the limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{5}+5x+3}{{x}^{4}-2}=$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503750 1072 #uuid "e026e16b-5d19-5563-a374-d9f17e04bfda" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503750 1074 "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{4}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{5}+5x+3}{{x}^{4}-2} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{5x}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(x+\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}})}{(1-\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\text{ }\\frac{x+0+0}{1-0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,x \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503751 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503631 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503751 1069 "q-237426" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503751 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503751 1071 "The measure of the angle that forms a linear pair with the angle of ${27}^{∘}$ is{{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503751 1072 #uuid "e08860ba-c89a-5bab-9100-e207695170f9" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503751 1074 "If two angles form a linear pair, the sum of their measures is equal to ${180}^{∘}.$ \n\nSo, the measure of an angle forming a linear pair with an angle of ${27}^{∘}$ equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}-{27}^{∘} & ={153}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503752 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503538 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503752 1069 "q-95601" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503752 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503752 1071 "$\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})=$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503752 1072 #uuid "ea77914f-6e86-5cb0-a566-942e82f36dc8" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503752 1074 "The definition of $\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x & =\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}-1}{{e}^{2x}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-2x}}{1+{e}^{-2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the third definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-2(-1/2)}}{1+{e}^{-2(-1/2)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-e}{1+e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503753 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503523 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503753 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503554 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503753 1069 "q-166761" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503753 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503753 1071 "Mary wonders where and when they'd meet if Sam left home $3.8$ minutes earlier and walked with a speed of $50$ meters per minute. She finds that the distance $d$ from home they'd meet can be modeled using the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}d+60t=960 \\\\ d-50t=190.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time (in minutes) since Mary left the community park.\n\nMary and Sam would meet$\\,$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$minutes after Mary left the community park.\n\nMary and Sam would meet at a distance of $\\,$\n{{field-2}}$\\,$meters from home." 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503753 1072 #uuid "ee041a5d-3530-58d3-9ad7-acdf10ca4984" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503753 1074 "To solve the given system of linear equations, we will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. It doesn't matter which equation we pick first, so we choose the easiest one. In this case, the second equation looks like the easiest.\n\nUsing the second equation, we can isolate $d$ adding to both sides of the equation $50t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d-50t=190 \\\\ d=190+50t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we replace $d$ with $190+50t$ in the first equation and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d+60t=960 \\\\ (190+50t)+60t & =960 \\\\ 190+110t & =960 \\\\ 110t & =960-190 \\\\ 110t & =770 \\\\ t & =\\frac{770}{110} \\\\ t & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, they would have met after $t=7$ minutes.\n\nIf we substitute $t=7$ into $d=190+50t$, we can find the value of $d:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =190+50t \\\\  & =190+50(7) \\\\  & =540\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, they meet at a distance of $540$ meters from home." 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503754 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503607 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503754 1069 "q-237185" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503754 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503754 1071 "![](images/78/78d588198d35e69ca7ae2b199dd52d2c07e5c9d3268214821b737deffe5cb03d.png)\n\nGiven that $\\overset{―}{CE}$ is a segment, what is the measure of $∠BOE?$\n\n$m∠BOE=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503754 1072 #uuid "f0518b35-857c-5bdb-a223-e7f6be6165f2" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503754 1074 "![](images/d3/d3ba77dd51c7d26685246304088d484144f5dcda57c424656ae305cc2ffa78ae.png)\n\nTwo angles are supplementary if their measures add up to ${180}^{∘},$ a straight angle.\n\nSince $\\overset{―}{CE}$ is a segment, we have that $∠COB$ and $∠BOE$ are supplementary. \n\n$$\nm∠COB+m∠BOE={180}^{∘}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the measure of $∠BOE$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠BOE & ={180}^{∘}-m∠COB \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{47}^{∘} \\\\  & ={133}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503755 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503555 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503755 1069 "q-139538" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503755 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503755 1071 "A $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=8$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=3\\sqrt{3},$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503755 1072 #uuid "f2c3caea-6032-5d20-9fea-52c78472efb1" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503755 1074 "The matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto the ellipse $\\{Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is ${\\sigma }_{1}=8,$ the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ must be ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}={8}^{2}.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax{‖}^{2}\\}={8}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax‖\\}=8.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is ${\\sigma }_{1}=8.$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503756 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503620 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503756 1069 "q-9445" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503756 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503756 1071 "$\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\sqrt{3})=$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503756 1072 #uuid "f8cf4372-3ba4-555f-93d2-c874764a61de" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503756 1074 "The definition of $\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x & =\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}-1}{{e}^{2x}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-2x}}{1+{e}^{-2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the second definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) & =\\frac{{e}^{2\\sqrt{3}}-1}{{e}^{2\\sqrt{3}}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503757 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503543 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503757 1069 "q-98337" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503757 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503757 1071 "![](images/50/50a06e9bb2865ea9ce3314700d89de3cd12139dee72ddcf2652fb868e88abbcb.png)\n\nWhich of the curves above is simple, closed, and negatively oriented?" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503757 1072 #uuid "fb704702-8992-5802-a3da-c6412351672c" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503757 1074 "A simple closed curve is positively oriented if, when traveling along it, one always has the curve interior to the left and the curve exterior to the right. Otherwise, the curve is negatively oriented. \n\nA curve is positively oriented if it is traversed counterclockwise and is negatively oriented if it is traversed clockwise.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the curves:\n\n- Curve I is simple, closed, negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed clockwise.\n\n- Curve II is simple, closed, negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed clockwise.\n\n- Curve III is not simple.\n\n- Curve IV is a simple and closed curve but it is not negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed counterclockwise, so it is positively oriented.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503758 1062 #uuid "01f99ab6-ea0a-570b-aeb6-695dc00afd24" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503758 1063 #edb/ref 17592186395500 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503759 1062 #uuid "0341db66-4a35-52c0-ad9b-5f93a0cf7225" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503759 1063 #edb/ref 17592186234323 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503760 1062 #uuid "053d77c3-8a49-5705-b184-df8c4f01cb7a" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503760 1063 #edb/ref 17592186503683 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503761 1062 #uuid "097cd705-286e-5518-8b5b-e2bba670d970" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503761 1063 #edb/ref 17592186233441 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503762 1058 #edb/ref 17592186503823 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503762 1062 #uuid "0bdd0386-927d-558d-9807-930f47803e99" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503762 1063 #edb/ref 17592186503722 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503763 1058 #edb/ref 17592186503840 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503763 1062 #uuid "0d6acb7c-f7cd-511c-b977-1c4f19622515" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503763 1063 #edb/ref 17592186503662 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503764 1062 #uuid "0dd12746-614b-56bd-8219-38b7f64cc9df" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503764 1063 #edb/ref 17592186503669 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503765 1058 #edb/ref 17592186503841 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503765 1062 #uuid "0fcd4859-f70e-5b53-af4b-3bf056bd41ce" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503765 1063 #edb/ref 17592186503727 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503766 1062 #uuid "1031e6a3-2568-506d-9850-2de85ed63b35" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503766 1063 #edb/ref 17592186503687 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503767 1058 #edb/ref 17592186503768 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503767 1062 #uuid "13e0f70b-698d-55d0-bc47-57987cc04fec" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503767 1063 #edb/ref 17592186503728 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503768 1058 #edb/ref 17592186503790 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503768 1062 #uuid "182aa6cf-2207-5cff-97ff-f13a3a95bbd4" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503768 1063 #edb/ref 17592186134395 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503769 1062 #uuid "19b316f6-8f9d-5a8d-adf5-5812ef547f3c" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503769 1063 #edb/ref 17592186233097 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503770 1058 #edb/ref 17592186503784 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503770 1062 #uuid "1b2ac5b1-579a-53c7-a81c-873a2e26baee" 1319413953543